\subsection{Enriching session papers}

We load the session, choose the decisions to work on, enrich them chunk
by chunk, and report.
Without [[--pending-only]] enrichment runs over \emph{all} the session's
papers, not only those missing an abstract: a paper can be
metadata-complete yet never have had its open-access status looked up,
and the per-paper cost for a complete paper is one cached (or skipped)
Unpaywall call.  The \enquote{N papers to enrich} message therefore
describes the metadata gaps, while the open-access and currency passes
cover every selected paper.

<<enrich session papers>>=
console = Console()
enrich_fields = parse_enrich_fields(fields)
providers_for_enrich = parse_enrich_providers(enrich_providers)

session_data = open_session_argument(session)
if session_data is None:
    console.print(f"Session not found: {escape(session)}")
    raise typer.Exit(1)

<<select the decisions to enrich>>
<<enrich and save chunk by chunk>>
<<report open-access findings during enrich>>
<<warn on currency issues during enrich>>
<<report the papers still without an abstract>>
<<report how many papers gained metadata>>
@

[[--pending-only]] selects by each decision's status as stored, not
through [[ReviewSession.pending_papers]], which keeps a cached list.
The restriction covers everything the command does to a paper:
metadata, Unpaywall, preprint adoption and the Crossref check.
A decided paper is not touched, and the saves write it back unchanged.

<<select the decisions to enrich>>=
from scholar.enrich import needs_enrichment
from scholar.review import DecisionStatus

selected = [
    decision
    for decision in session_data.decisions
    if not pending_only or decision.status == DecisionStatus.PENDING
]
scope = "pending papers" if pending_only else "papers"
papers_to_enrich = [
    d.paper for d in selected if needs_enrichment(d.paper, enrich_fields)
]

of_what = f"{len(selected)} " + ("pending papers" if pending_only else "total")
if papers_to_enrich:
    console.print(
        f"[cyan]Found {len(papers_to_enrich)} papers to enrich "
        f"(of {of_what}).[/cyan]"
    )
else:
    console.print(
        f"[yellow]No {scope} need metadata enrichment "
        f"(all have {', '.join(enrich_fields)}).[/yellow]"
    )
@

\subsection{Saving chunk by chunk}
\label{sec:enrich-chunks}

A run over a large session takes long enough to be cut short---by a
time limit, a closed terminal or [[Ctrl-C]]---and the command used to
save only at the very end, so an interrupted run kept nothing but the
provider caches.
We therefore work in chunks of [[ENRICH_SAVE_EVERY]] papers.
Each chunk goes through the whole pipeline---enrichment (with one
OpenAlex prefetch, \cref{sec:openalex-doi-batch}), the Crossref check,
the preprint-adoption bookkeeping---and then the session is saved.
Saving often is safe because [[save_session]] merges what other
processes saved meanwhile and then takes the saved state as its new
baseline (\cref{sec:session-integrity}): each save compares against the
previous one, not against the state loaded hours ago.
The price is the backup: [[.bak]] keeps one generation, so after a
chunked run it holds the state before the last chunk, not the state
before the run.

Fifty papers is one OpenAlex batch, and at the slowest pace of a run
(Semantic Scholar without a key, one request in three seconds) a few
minutes of work, which bounds what an interruption can lose.

<<constants>>=
ENRICH_SAVE_EVERY = 50  # papers per chunk; the session is saved after each
@

An interruption ends the loop with [[KeyboardInterrupt]].
We catch it, say how many papers were saved, and exit with 130, the
status bash gives a command ended by SIGINT (128 plus the signal
number, according to its manual page).
A time limit sends SIGTERM instead (the default of [[timeout]], per its
manual page), and Python does not handle SIGTERM: the process ends on
the spot, and its exit handlers do not run---we checked, with a
script that registers one and sends itself the signal.
An exit handler is what writes the provider caches to disk
([[save_all_caches]], \cref{cache-module}), so a run
ended that way would lose even the lookups it paid for.
While the loop runs we therefore turn SIGTERM into the same
[[KeyboardInterrupt]]; the same script with such a handler exits
through [[sys.exit(130)]] and runs its exit handler.
The work of the chunk in progress is lost either way, apart from what
the providers cached.
The papers already processed are in [[originals]] and [[processed]],
and the outcomes of their lookups in [[lookup_outcomes]]
(\cref{sec:enrich-lookup-tally}), which the reports below use.

<<enrich and save chunk by chunk>>=
from collections import Counter

from rich.progress import Progress
from scholar.notes import get_paper_id, migrate_paper_id
from scholar.review import keep_copy_before_run

originals: list = []
processed: list = []
lookup_outcomes: list = []
crossref_failures: Counter = Counter()
adopted_any = False
pre_run_copy = (
    keep_copy_before_run(session_data, "pre-enrich") if selected else None
)
try:
    with sigterm_as_keyboard_interrupt(), Progress() as progress:
        task = progress.add_task("[cyan]Enriching...", total=len(selected))
        for start in range(0, len(selected), ENRICH_SAVE_EVERY):
            chunk = selected[start:start + ENRICH_SAVE_EVERY]
            <<enrich, check and save one chunk>>
except KeyboardInterrupt:
    console.print(
        f"[yellow]Interrupted: saved {len(processed)} of {len(selected)} "
        f"{scope}; run again to continue.[/yellow]"
    )
    <<report the papers still without an abstract>>
    <<say where the pre-run copy is>>
    raise typer.Exit(130)
@

Since [[.bak]] ends up holding only the state before the last chunk, we
first keep a copy of the session as it was before the run
([[keep_copy_before_run]], \cref{sec:session-integrity}), unless there is
nothing to enrich and so no save to follow.
It is the way back from the whole run, which matters because enrichment
replaces metadata, and, when it adopts a preprint, a paper's id.
Each run makes a new timestamped copy and the newest three are kept
([[RUN_COPIES_KEPT]]), so that continuing an interrupted run does not
overwrite the original.
The copy covers the session file only: an adoption also moves the notes
and decision records to the new id ([[migrate_paper_id]]), so the report
adds a line saying that restoring does not undo that, when the run
adopted a preprint.
The final report and the interrupt message both name it, with the path
written out so that it can be pasted into a [[cp]] command.

<<say where the pre-run copy is>>=
if pre_run_copy is not None:
    console.print(
        "[dim]Session as it was before this run: "
        f"{escape(str(pre_run_copy))}[/dim]",
        soft_wrap=True,
    )
    if adopted_any:
        console.print(
            "[dim]Restoring it does not undo the notes and decision "
            "records moved to the adopted ids.[/dim]"
        )
@

Within a chunk the order matters: the Crossref check reads the DOI the
enrichment settled on (an adopted preprint's, if any), and the ids are
migrated before the save so that the notes and decisions stored under
the old id are moved by the time the session names the new one.

<<enrich, check and save one chunk>>=
from scholar.enrich import enrich_papers
from scholar.crossref import annotate_papers, noting_failures
from scholar.review import save_session

before = [decision.paper for decision in chunk]
chunk_outcomes: list = []
after = enrich_papers(
    before,
    fields=enrich_fields,
    providers=providers_for_enrich,
    progress_callback=lambda current, _total: progress.update(
        task, completed=start + current
    ),
    preprint_search=preprint_search,
    outcomes=chunk_outcomes,
)
after = annotate_papers(
    after, progress_callback=noting_failures(crossref_failures)
)
for decision, paper in zip(chunk, after):
    decision.paper = paper
<<record preprint adoptions during enrich>>
save_session(session_data)
originals.extend(before)
processed.extend(after)
lookup_outcomes.extend(chunk_outcomes)
@

[[signal.signal]] may only be called from the main thread, and the
command can be called from elsewhere (a worker thread of a test runner
or an application); there we leave SIGTERM alone rather than fail.

<<enrich command>>=
@contextlib.contextmanager
def sigterm_as_keyboard_interrupt():
    """Raise `KeyboardInterrupt` on SIGTERM while the block runs."""

    def interrupt(signum, frame):
        raise KeyboardInterrupt

    try:
        previous = signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, interrupt)
    except ValueError:  # not the main thread
        yield
        return
    try:
        yield
    finally:
        signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, previous)
<<cli imports>>=
import contextlib
import signal
@

When the preprint fallback (\cref{preprint-module}) adopted a preprint,
the paper's identity changed---and the notes and decision stores are
keyed by that identity, so records saved under the published DOI would
be orphaned.  Adoption is detectable by comparing ids across the
enrichment: [[enrich_paper]] is the only enrichment step that can
change [[Paper.id]].  For each adoption we migrate the persistent
stores ([[migrate_paper_id]], \cref{notes-module}) and tell the user
what was swapped, naming both DOIs so the substitution is transparent
in the terminal transcript, not only in the session file.

<<record preprint adoptions during enrich>>=
for orig, new in zip(before, after):
    if get_paper_id(orig) == get_paper_id(new):
        continue
    migrate_paper_id(get_paper_id(orig), get_paper_id(new))
    adopted_any = True
    console.print(
        f"[cyan]Adopted preprint {new.doi} for "
        f"\N{LEFT DOUBLE QUOTATION MARK}{escape(new.title_preview())}"
        f"\N{RIGHT DOUBLE QUOTATION MARK} "
        f"(published DOI {orig.doi} kept for provenance).[/cyan]"
    )
@

The open-access tally answers the question a reviewer actually has---how much
of this session can I read?---so we count open papers and, among them, those
with a direct PDF link, over the papers Unpaywall managed to check.  When no
contact email is configured the lookups were silently skipped, and the only
useful thing to say is which variable enables them; we say it once here rather
than once per paper.  When [[pdf_url]] was not among the fields, no lookup was
made at all, and a tally would only repeat the findings of earlier runs.
The tally counts the papers this run processed: with [[--pending-only]] the
pending ones, and after an interruption none, since the command has exited.

<<report open-access findings during enrich>>=
from scholar import unpaywall

if "pdf_url" not in enrich_fields:
    console.print(
        "[dim]Open-access lookup skipped: pdf_url is not among "
        "--fields.[/dim]"
    )
elif unpaywall.is_configured():
    checked = [p for p in processed if p.oa_status is not None]
    open_papers = [p for p in checked if p.is_open_access]
    with_pdf = [p for p in open_papers if p.pdf_url]
    console.print(
        f"[cyan]Open access: {len(open_papers)} of {len(checked)} checked "
        f"{scope} ({len(with_pdf)} with PDF link).[/cyan]"
    )
    <<say why open-access statuses are still unknown>>
else:
    console.print(
        "[dim]Unpaywall lookup skipped: set SCHOLAR_EMAIL to enable "
        "open-access checks.[/dim]"
    )
@

Enrichment is also the natural moment to check currency: we already hold the
papers and are about to write them back, so a retraction check costs one
extra pass and saves its results in the same [[save_session]].  We check
\emph{every} selected paper, not just those that needed metadata
enrichment---a paper can be complete yet retracted---so the check runs on
each chunk as a whole rather than on the [[papers_to_enrich]] subset.

We only surface problems, once at the end and over every paper the run
processed (a warning per retracted, corrected, or superseded paper); the clean
and unchecked majority stay silent.  Users who want the full per-paper picture
run [[scholar verify]] instead.

<<warn on currency issues during enrich>>=
warnings = [
    text
    for paper in processed
    if (text := format_currency_warning(paper)) is not None
]
if warnings:
    console.print(f"[red]Found {len(warnings)} currency issue(s):[/red]")
    for text in warnings:
        console.print(text)
else:
    console.print("[green]No retractions or corrections found.[/green]")
<<say how many currency checks failed>>
@

\enquote{No retractions or corrections found} speaks only for the
papers Crossref answered for.
A check that failed leaves its paper unchecked
([[annotate_papers]], \cref{crossref-module}), and a run whose checks
all failed, as they do in an environment without a CA bundle, used to
end with that line and nothing else.
So the chunks count the failed checks per reason ([[noting_failures]]
wraps the check's progress callback, \cref{sec:crossref-check-failures})
and the report adds [[check_failure_lines]] after it: how many papers'
checks failed, why, and that running again retries them.
The run itself goes on, as it always did; the tally is what makes the
failures visible.

<<say how many currency checks failed>>=
from scholar.crossref import check_failure_lines

for line in check_failure_lines(crossref_failures, scope):
    console.print(escape(line), style="yellow", soft_wrap=True)
@

\subsection{Why lookups found nothing}
\label{sec:enrich-lookup-tally}

A paper still without an abstract after the run is either one that no
database asked has an abstract for, or one whose lookup failed and may
succeed on another run; a review that excludes records for lack of an
abstract must not treat the second kind as the first
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/259}).
The command therefore collects the outcome of every paper's lookups
([[LookupOutcome]], \cref{sec:enrich-lookup-outcomes}) beside the
papers themselves, chunk by chunk, and ends with their tally
([[abstract_tally_lines]]): how many papers still lack an abstract, how
many of those every database asked answered for, how many had no DOI,
and how many had a failed lookup, per database and reason, with a hint
to run again only when there are such.
The interrupt path prints the same tally over the papers saved so far,
since those are the ones a second run would continue from; without an
abstract among [[--fields]] no lookup was made for one and nothing is
said.
The lines are printed escaped and soft-wrapped: a reason is the
service's or the limiter's text and may hold brackets, which [[rich]]
would read as markup.

<<report the papers still without an abstract>>=
if "abstract" in enrich_fields and processed:
    from scholar.enrich import abstract_tally_lines, tally_lookups

    lookup_tally = tally_lookups(processed, lookup_outcomes)
    for line in abstract_tally_lines(lookup_tally, scope):
        console.print(
            escape(line),
            style="yellow" if lookup_tally.with_failure else "cyan",
            soft_wrap=True,
        )
@

The open-access tally above counts the checked papers only.
The others are unknown for one of three reasons, which
[[open_access_tally_lines]] tells apart: no DOI to look up, no record in
Unpaywall, or a failed lookup, counted per reason
(\cref{unpaywall-module}).
Only the last is worth a second run, and only then is the hint given.

<<say why open-access statuses are still unknown>>=
from scholar.enrich import open_access_tally_lines, tally_lookups

for line in open_access_tally_lines(
    tally_lookups(processed, lookup_outcomes), scope
):
    console.print(escape(line), style="cyan", soft_wrap=True)
@

Let's verify both tallies through the real command and the real
enrichment.
The stand-ins are at the network's edge, as in the tests of
\cref{sec:enrich-chunks}: the Crossref check passes the papers
through, and the providers are reduced to one real Semantic Scholar
provider whose limiter is in a cooldown and whose cache holds the
answer \enquote{no such paper} for the second paper's DOI.
The first paper's lookup is thus refused before any request, the way a
throttled run's is, and the second is answered, the way a 404 is.
The abstract services Crossref and Europe PMC
(\cref{sec:enrichment-providers}) are switched off here; the next tests
are theirs.
For the open-access tally the real Unpaywall client is used the same
way, and the preprint fallback, which would ask OpenAlex and Crossref,
finds nothing.

<<test functions>>=
class TestEnrichLookupTally:
  """`scholar enrich` says why papers still lack an abstract (#259)."""

  REASON = "cooldown for the tally test"

  @staticmethod
  def _throttled_s2(monkeypatch, answered_doi):
    from scholar.providers import SemanticScholarProvider

    provider = SemanticScholarProvider(api_key=None)
    monkeypatch.setattr(provider, "_cache", {("doi", answered_doi): None})
    provider._limiter.record_limit_hit(
      retry_after=3600, reason=TestEnrichLookupTally.REASON
    )
    return provider

  @staticmethod
  def _invoke(args, provider):
    with patch(
      "scholar.enrich.get_provider",
      lambda name: provider if name == "s2" else None,
    ), patch("scholar.enrich.abstract_source", lambda name: None), patch(
      "scholar.crossref.annotate_papers",
      side_effect=lambda papers, **kw: list(papers),
    ):
      return runner.invoke(app, ["enrich", *args])

  <<enrich lookup tally test methods>>
@

A refused and an answered lookup end in different rows, the old lines
stay, and the hint to run again is there:

<<enrich lookup tally test methods>>=
def test_refused_and_answered_are_told_apart(self, tmp_path, monkeypatch):
  name = TestEnrichSelectionAndSaving._session(tmp_path, monkeypatch, 2)
  s2 = self._throttled_s2(monkeypatch, "10.1/c137-1")
  result = self._invoke([name, "--fields", "abstract"], s2)
  assert result.exit_code == 0, result.stdout
  out = result.stdout
  assert "Still without an abstract: 2 of 2 papers." in out
  assert "1 answered by every database asked" in out
  assert "1 with a failed lookup, by database and reason:" in out
  assert f"s2: {self.REASON}" in out
  assert "Run again to retry the failed lookups." in out
  assert "Enriched 0 papers." in out
@

An interruption prints the tally of the papers saved before it.
The provider is wrapped so that the second paper's lookup is where the
interruption happens:

<<enrich lookup tally test methods>>=
def test_interrupt_prints_the_tally(self, tmp_path, monkeypatch):
  monkeypatch.setattr("scholar.cli.ENRICH_SAVE_EVERY", 1)
  name = TestEnrichSelectionAndSaving._session(tmp_path, monkeypatch, 2)
  s2 = self._throttled_s2(monkeypatch, "10.1/unused")

  class InterruptedAtSecond:
    @property
    def last_lookup_error(self):
      return s2.last_lookup_error

    def get_paper_by_doi(self, doi):
      if doi == "10.1/c137-1":
        raise KeyboardInterrupt
      return s2.get_paper_by_doi(doi)

  result = self._invoke(
    [name, "--fields", "abstract"], InterruptedAtSecond()
  )
  assert result.exit_code == 130
  out = result.stdout
  assert "Interrupted: saved 1 of 2 papers" in out
  assert "Still without an abstract: 1 of 1 papers." in out
  assert f"s2: {self.REASON}" in out
@

The open-access part separates a DOI Unpaywall answered for without a
record (a cached 404) from one whose lookup was refused:

<<enrich lookup tally test methods>>=
def test_open_access_unknown_reasons(self, tmp_path, monkeypatch):
  from scholar import unpaywall

  name = TestEnrichSelectionAndSaving._session(tmp_path, monkeypatch, 2)
  monkeypatch.setenv("SCHOLAR_EMAIL", "tests@example.org")
  monkeypatch.setitem(unpaywall._client._cache, ("oa", "10.1/c137-1"), None)
  unpaywall._client._limiter.record_limit_hit(
    retry_after=3600, reason=self.REASON
  )
  monkeypatch.setattr(
    "scholar.preprint.find_preprint", lambda paper, search=False: None
  )
  result = self._invoke([name, "--fields", "pdf_url"], None)
  assert result.exit_code == 0, result.stdout
  out = result.stdout
  assert "Open access: 0 of 0 checked papers (0 with PDF link)." in out
  assert (
    "Open-access status unknown for 2 papers: 1 not in Unpaywall, "
    "1 with a failed lookup." in out
  )
  assert f"unpaywall: {self.REASON}" in out
  assert "Still without an abstract" not in out
@

Last, the failure reported from a real run: an environment that can
make no HTTPS request at all.
[[REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE]] pointing at a file that does not exist makes
every request through [[requests]] raise the same [[OSError]] in
[[HTTPAdapter.cert_verify]], before a connection is opened, so the
whole run is real down to that point: Semantic Scholar and OpenAlex
(fresh providers with empty caches, OpenAlex's batch included),
Unpaywall and the Crossref check.
Each service must be named with that reason in the tallies, the run
must finish (exit 0), and the papers must be saved as never checked by
Crossref.

<<enrich lookup tally test methods>>=
def test_broken_tls_setup_is_named_for_every_service(
  self, tmp_path, monkeypatch
):
  from scholar import crossref, unpaywall
  from scholar.providers import OpenAlexProvider, SemanticScholarProvider

  bundle = "/nonexistent/scholar-test/ca.pem"
  name = TestEnrichSelectionAndSaving._session(tmp_path, monkeypatch, 2)
  monkeypatch.setenv("REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE", bundle)
  monkeypatch.setenv("SCHOLAR_EMAIL", "tests@example.org")
  monkeypatch.setattr(unpaywall._client, "_cache", {})
  monkeypatch.setattr(crossref._client, "_cache", {})
  monkeypatch.setattr(
    "scholar.preprint.find_preprint", lambda paper, search=False: None
  )
  providers = {
    "s2": SemanticScholarProvider(api_key=None),
    "openalex": OpenAlexProvider(api_key=None),
  }
  for provider in providers.values():
    monkeypatch.setattr(provider, "_cache", {})
  with patch("scholar.enrich.get_provider", providers.get):
    result = runner.invoke(app, [
      "enrich", name, "--fields", "abstract,pdf_url",
      "--enrich-providers", "s2,openalex",
    ])
  assert result.exit_code == 0, result.stdout
  out = result.stdout
  reason = (
    "Could not find a suitable TLS CA certificate bundle, invalid path: "
    + bundle
  )
  for service in ("s2", "openalex", "unpaywall", "crossref"):
    assert f"{service}: {reason} (2)" in out, service
  assert "2 with a failed lookup, by database and reason:" in out
  assert "Crossref check failed for 2 papers" in out
  saved = TestEnrichSelectionAndSaving._saved(name)
  assert [d.paper.updated_by for d in saved.values()] == [None, None]
@

A database that declines is information, not a failure.
Off campus Scopus has no abstracts to give and asks nothing
(\cref{sec:scopus-doi-lookup}); a run there must still read the papers
every other database answered for as settled, name Scopus once with
its reason, and not tell the user to run again.

<<enrich lookup tally test methods>>=
def test_declining_scopus_is_not_a_failure(self, tmp_path, monkeypatch):
  from scholar.providers import ScopusProvider, SemanticScholarProvider

  name = TestEnrichSelectionAndSaving._session(tmp_path, monkeypatch, 2)
  monkeypatch.setenv("SCOPUS_VIEW", "standard")
  s2 = SemanticScholarProvider(api_key=None)
  monkeypatch.setattr(s2, "_cache", {
    ("doi", "10.1/c137-0"): None, ("doi", "10.1/c137-1"): None,
  })
  providers = {"s2": s2, "scopus": ScopusProvider(api_key="dummy")}
  with patch("scholar.enrich.get_provider", providers.get), patch(
    "scholar.crossref.annotate_papers",
    side_effect=lambda papers, **kw: list(papers),
  ):
    result = runner.invoke(app, [
      "enrich", name, "--fields", "abstract",
      "--enrich-providers", "s2,scopus",
    ])
  assert result.exit_code == 0, result.stdout
  out = result.stdout
  assert "2 answered by every database asked" in out
  assert (
    "scopus not asked: SCOPUS_VIEW=standard has no abstracts (2 papers)"
