\section{Verifying currency}
\label{sec:verify-command}

Where [[enrich]] checks currency as a side effect, [[verify]] makes it the
point: it reports the status of \emph{every} paper in a session, not just the
problem ones, and offers a [[--json]] mode so the [[backing-claims]] skill can
consume the result programmatically.  It shares [[enrich]]'s argument shape (a
session name with the same completion) so the two commands feel like a pair.

<<verify command>>=
@app.command()
def verify(
    session: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Argument(
            help="Name of the review session to check.",
            autocompletion=complete_session_name,
        ),
    ],
    json_output: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(
            "--json",
            help="Emit the currency report as a JSON array.",
        ),
    ] = False,
) -> None:
    """
    Check a session's papers for retractions, corrections, and newer versions.

    Looks up each paper's DOI in Crossref and reports whether it has been
    retracted or corrected, or has a superseding version. The discovered
    status is saved back to the session.

    Example:
        scholar verify "machine learning"
    """
    <<verify session currency>>
@

We borrow the stderr-console discipline introduced for [[pdf quote]]
(\cref{ch:pdf-cli}): all progress and diagnostics go to [[err_console]], so
in [[--json]] mode standard output carries nothing but the JSON array and a
consumer can pipe it straight into a parser.  A missing session is therefore an
error on stderr plus a non-zero exit, never a stray line on stdout.

<<verify session currency>>=
from collections import Counter

from scholar.review import save_session
from scholar.crossref import (
    annotate_papers,
    check_failure_lines,
    currency_status,
    noting_failures,
)
from rich.progress import Progress

console = Console()
err_console = Console(stderr=True)

session_data = open_session_argument(session)
if session_data is None:
    err_console.print(f"[red]Session not found:[/red] {escape(session)}")
    raise typer.Exit(1)

papers = [decision.paper for decision in session_data.decisions]
<<run the currency check with a progress bar>>

# Persist the discovered status back to the session.
for decision, paper in zip(session_data.decisions, checked):
    decision.paper = paper
save_session(session_data)
<<say on stderr how many checks failed>>

<<output the currency report>>
@

A paper whose check failed reads \enquote{unknown} in the report, like
one without a DOI, so the failures are counted as [[enrich]] counts
them (\cref{sec:crossref-check-failures}) and said once, per reason.
They go to [[err_console]] in both modes: they are a diagnostic of the
run, like the progress bar, and the [[--json]] output must stay pure.

<<say on stderr how many checks failed>>=
for line in check_failure_lines(crossref_failures, "papers"):
    err_console.print(escape(line), style="yellow", soft_wrap=True)
@

The progress bar is attached to [[err_console]] for the same reason: a Rich
progress display writes escape sequences that must not contaminate JSON output.
The per-paper callback drives it as Crossref is queried one DOI at a time.

<<run the currency check with a progress bar>>=
with Progress(console=err_console) as progress:
    task = progress.add_task(
        "[cyan]Checking Crossref...", total=len(papers)
    )

    def update_progress(done: int, total: int) -> None:
        progress.update(task, completed=done)

    crossref_failures: Counter = Counter()
    checked = annotate_papers(
        papers,
        progress_callback=noting_failures(crossref_failures, update_progress),
    )
@

The JSON shape mirrors what a verification checklist needs: the DOI and title to
identify the paper, the one-word [[status]], and the raw notice and
newer-version lists so a caller can show the linking DOIs without a second
lookup.  [[published_versions]] is included so an adopted preprint's
provenance (which published DOI it replaced) travels with its
\enquote{preprint} status.  An empty session is a valid empty array,
not a message.

<<output the currency report>>=
if json_output:
    report = [
        {
            "doi": paper.doi,
            "title": paper.title,
            "status": currency_status(paper),
            "updated_by": paper.updated_by,
            "newer_versions": paper.newer_versions,
            "published_versions": paper.published_versions,
        }
        for paper in checked
    ]
    print(json.dumps(report, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2))
    return

<<print the human currency report>>
@

In human mode we lead with the problems---each rendered by the shared
[[format_currency_warning]] helper---and close with a one-line tally of every
status so the reader can see the clean and unchecked counts too.
Between the two sit the adopted preprints: they are deliberate
substitutions, not problems, so they render as dim informational lines
(\enquote{preprint of \dots}, naming the published DOI) rather than
warnings, and get their own slot in the tally.

<<print the human currency report>>=
from collections import Counter

problems = [
    text
    for paper in checked
    if (text := format_currency_warning(paper)) is not None
]
if problems:
    for text in problems:
        console.print(text)
else:
    console.print(
        "[green]No retractions, corrections, or newer versions found.[/green]"
    )

for paper in checked:
    if currency_status(paper) != "preprint":
        continue
    published_dois = ", ".join(
        entry["doi"]
        for entry in paper.published_versions or []
        if entry.get("doi")
    )
    console.print(
        f"[dim]\\[PREPRINT] {escape(paper.title_preview())} "
        f"-> preprint of {published_dois}[/dim]"
    )

counts = Counter(currency_status(paper) for paper in checked)
console.print(
    f"[bold]{len(checked)} papers[/bold]: "
    f"{counts.get('retracted', 0)} retracted, "
    f"{counts.get('corrected', 0)} corrected, "
    f"{counts.get('superseded', 0)} superseded, "
    f"{counts.get('preprint', 0)} preprint, "
    f"{counts.get('ok', 0)} ok, "
    f"{counts.get('unknown', 0)} unknown"
)
@

Let's verify the command end to end: the human report, the JSON mode, and the
missing-session error.  We build a small session on disk and stub the Crossref
lookup so no network is touched---the stub marks one paper retracted and leaves
the other clean.

<<test functions>>=
class TestEnrichCommand:
    """Tests for the enrich command's open-access and currency passes."""

    def _make_session(self, tmp_path, monkeypatch):
        """Create a two-paper session (one complete, one bare) on disk."""
        from scholar import Paper, SearchResult
        from scholar.review import create_review_session, save_session

        monkeypatch.setenv("SCHOLAR_DATA_DIR", str(tmp_path))
        papers = [
            Paper(
                title="Full", authors=["a"], doi="10.1/full", sources=["m"],
                abstract="Has one", pdf_url="https://p/full.pdf",
                keywords=["k"],
            ),
            Paper(title="Bare", authors=["b"], doi="10.1/bare", sources=["m"]),
        ]
        result = SearchResult(
            query="q", provider="m", timestamp="t", papers=papers
        )
        session = create_review_session([result], "q", session_name="etest")
        save_session(session)
        return "etest"

    @staticmethod
    def _fake_enrich(
        papers, fields=None, providers=None, progress_callback=None,
        preprint_search=False, outcomes=None,
    ):
        """Stand in for enrich_papers: mark everything gold OA with a PDF."""
        from dataclasses import replace

        return [
            replace(
                p, oa_status="gold", oa_url=f"https://j/{p.doi}",
                pdf_url=p.pdf_url or f"https://j/{p.doi}.pdf",
            )
            for p in papers
        ]

    @staticmethod
    def _identity_annotate(papers, progress_callback=None):
        return list(papers)

    def test_reports_open_access_and_persists(self, tmp_path, monkeypatch):
        """The OA tally is printed and the new links are saved."""
        from scholar import isolated_registry
        from scholar.review import load_session

        name = self._make_session(tmp_path, monkeypatch)
        monkeypatch.setenv("SCHOLAR_EMAIL", "tests@example.org")
        with patch(
            "scholar.enrich.enrich_papers", side_effect=self._fake_enrich
        ), patch(
            "scholar.crossref.annotate_papers",
            side_effect=self._identity_annotate,
        ):
            result = runner.invoke(app, ["enrich", name])
        assert result.exit_code == 0, result.stdout
        assert "Open access: 2 of 2 checked papers (2 with PDF link)" in result.stdout
        assert "Enriched 1 papers" in result.stdout
        # Reload in a fresh registry: the process-wide one still holds the
        # Paper objects created above and would hand them back unchanged.
        with isolated_registry():
            saved = load_session(name)
        by_doi = {d.paper.doi: d.paper for d in saved.decisions}
        assert by_doi["10.1/bare"].pdf_url == "https://j/10.1/bare.pdf"
        assert by_doi["10.1/bare"].oa_status == "gold"
        assert by_doi["10.1/full"].pdf_url == "https://p/full.pdf"

    def test_hint_when_no_contact_email(self, tmp_path, monkeypatch):
        """Without SCHOLAR_EMAIL the command says how to enable lookups."""
        name = self._make_session(tmp_path, monkeypatch)
        with patch(
            "scholar.enrich.enrich_papers", side_effect=lambda ps, **kw: list(ps)
        ), patch(
            "scholar.crossref.annotate_papers",
            side_effect=self._identity_annotate,
        ):
            result = runner.invoke(app, ["enrich", name])
        assert result.exit_code == 0, result.stdout
        assert "set SCHOLAR_EMAIL" in result.stdout

    def test_complete_session_still_runs_checks(self, tmp_path, monkeypatch):
        """Even with nothing to fill in, the currency pass runs and saves."""
        from scholar import Paper, SearchResult
        from scholar.review import create_review_session, save_session

        monkeypatch.setenv("SCHOLAR_DATA_DIR", str(tmp_path))
        paper = Paper(
            title="Full", authors=["a"], doi="10.1/full", sources=["m"],
            abstract="Has one", pdf_url="https://p/full.pdf", keywords=["k"],
        )
        result = SearchResult(query="q", provider="m", timestamp="t", papers=[paper])
        save_session(create_review_session([result], "q", session_name="ctest"))
        with patch(
            "scholar.enrich.enrich_papers", side_effect=lambda ps, **kw: list(ps)
        ), patch(
            "scholar.crossref.annotate_papers",
            side_effect=self._identity_annotate,
        ) as mock_annotate:
            result = runner.invoke(app, ["enrich", "ctest"])
        assert result.exit_code == 0, result.stdout
        assert "No papers need metadata enrichment" in result.stdout
        mock_annotate.assert_called_once()

    def test_adoption_reports_and_migrates(self, tmp_path, monkeypatch):
        """An adopted preprint is announced and its records migrated."""
        from dataclasses import replace

        name = self._make_session(tmp_path, monkeypatch)

        def adopt_bare(papers, **kwargs):
            out = []
            for p in papers:
                if p.doi == "10.1/bare":
                    out.append(
                        replace(
                            p,
                            doi="10.48550/arXiv.2401.1",
                            pdf_url="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.1",
                            published_versions=[
                                {
                                    "doi": "10.1/bare",
                                    "relation": "preprint-of",
