\section{Query Syntax Reference}
\label{ch:syntax-cli}

Different providers support different query syntax features.
The [[syntax]] command displays a reference showing what each provider supports.

<<syntax command>>=
@app.command()
def syntax() -> None:
    """
    Show query syntax supported by each provider.

    Different providers support different features like boolean operators,
    wildcards, and phrase search. Use this command to see what's available.
    """
    <<display syntax reference>>
@

We display the information in a table for easy comparison.
Semantic Scholar's rows describe two endpoints
(\cref{sec:s2-two-searches} in the providers chapter): a query with
operators or quotes, or a search without a limit, goes to its bulk
search, which reads the operators, stems the terms and matches title
and abstract only; plain words with a limit go to the relevance
search, which stops at a thousand results.
The bulk search does not rank by relevance: with a limit it returns
the most cited matches first, and without one it stops at a safety cap
of a hundred thousand papers.
Its documentation states no precedence between its AND and OR, hence
the advice to group them; hyphenated words are sent quoted, and a
query in another database's field syntax is not sent at all.

