\subsection{Which fields a query searches}

The same query matches different parts of a paper on different
providers, which makes counts incomparable without the user knowing it
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/135}).  A third table
therefore says what each provider searches by default and what
[[--in title-abstract]] does there (\cref{sec:search-in} in the
[[scholar]] module).  Every entry comes from the provider's own
documentation, cited with the providers
(\cref{sec:providers-search-in} in the [[providers]] module):
OpenAlex's search page (title, abstract, full text and keywords),
Clarivate's Web of Science Starter API reference ([[TS]]: title,
abstract, author keywords, Keywords Plus), IEEE's Metadata API page
([[querytext]]: all configured metadata fields and the abstract) and
HAL's search API page (the [[text]] index, several fields).  Where the
documentation says nothing about the default---Semantic Scholar's
relevance search, Scopus without a field code, arXiv without a field
prefix---the table says so rather than guess (Semantic Scholar's bulk
search, which Boolean queries go to, is documented as matching title
and abstract, \cref{sec:s2-two-searches} in the providers chapter),
and DBLP's entry is
marked unverified because its documentation pages could not be read
when this was written.

