\section{Cache Management}
\label{ch:cache-cli}

Search results are cached to avoid redundant API calls.
Users can inspect and manage the cache using the [[cache]] command.

\subsection{Action-Based Design}

We use an action argument ([[clear]], [[info]], [[path]]) rather than
subcommands for cache management. This keeps the interface simpler for
a small set of related operations:
\begin{description}
\item[clear] Delete all cached results---useful when API data has changed
\item[info] Show statistics---helpful for debugging or quota planning
\item[path] Print directory---for scripting or manual inspection
\end{description}

If cache management grew more complex (e.g., expiration policies), we
might refactor to subcommands like the [[notes]] command uses.

\subsection{Clearing one provider's cache}

[[clear --provider NAME]] (repeatable) deletes only [[NAME.pkl]].
The cache never expires, so a single stale cache can be the problem
while the rest is worth keeping: results cached off the subscribing
network lack the abstracts Scopus returns on it, and the Scopus
provider points the user here (\cref{sec:scopus-cache-doubt}).
The names are those of the search and lookup caches that
[[cache info]] lists, that is the registered providers plus the
[[crossref]], [[europepmc]] and [[unpaywall]] lookup caches; the LLM caches it also
lists are cleared only by [[--include-llm]].
We check them against that set, since a typo would otherwise clear
nothing and look like success, and exit with status 2 (a usage error)
listing the valid names.
[[--provider]] names exactly what is deleted, so it is refused together
with [[--include-llm]], which would widen it; the same goes for the
other actions, where it would be silently ignored.

<<cache command>>=
def cache_provider_names() -> list[str]:
    """Names accepted by `cache clear --provider`, sorted."""
    from scholar.providers import PROVIDERS

    return sorted(set(PROVIDERS) | {"crossref", "europepmc", "unpaywall"})
@

\subsection{Keeping the LLM caches}

[[clear]] keeps the caches of paid language-model output (the synthesis
caches, \cref{cache-module}) and the [[--include-llm]] flag removes them
too.
A silent keep would surprise the user who expected an empty cache, so
[[clear]] says how many it kept and names the flag that removes them.
[[info]] lists them on a line of their own for the same reason: the user
can see what a plain [[clear]] leaves behind before running it.
The flag is an option of the whole command rather than an argument of
[[clear]] only, since the action is a plain string argument; it is
ignored by the other actions.

<<cache command>>=
@app.command()
def cache(
    action: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Argument(
            help="Action: clear, info, or path",
            autocompletion=complete_cache_action,
        ),
    ],
    include_llm: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(
            "--include-llm",
            help="With clear: also delete the LLM synthesis caches "
            "(paid model output, kept by default)",
        ),
    ] = False,
    provider: Annotated[
        Optional[list[str]],
        typer.Option(
            "--provider",
            help="With clear: delete only this provider's cache "
            "(repeatable; not with --include-llm)",
        ),
    ] = None,
) -> None:
    """
    Manage the search result cache.

    Actions:
        clear  - Delete cached search results (LLM caches kept
                 unless --include-llm; --provider NAME limits it
                 to one provider's cache)
        info   - Show cache statistics (entries, size, location)
        path   - Print the cache directory path
    """
    from scholar.cache import clear_cache, get_cache_stats, llm_cache_files

    console = Console()

    if provider and (action != "clear" or include_llm):
        console.print(
            "[red]--provider only works with clear, and not with "
            "--include-llm.[/red]"
        )
        raise typer.Exit(2)
    if provider:
        valid = cache_provider_names()
        unknown = [name for name in provider if name not in valid]
        if unknown:
            console.print(
                f"[red]Unknown provider cache: {', '.join(unknown)}."
                f"[/red] Valid names: {', '.join(valid)}."
            )
            raise typer.Exit(2)

    if action == "clear" and provider:
        count = clear_cache(providers=provider)
        console.print(f"Cleared {count} cache file(s).")

    elif action == "clear":
        count = clear_cache(include_llm=include_llm)
        console.print(f"Cleared {count} cache file(s).")
        kept = len(llm_cache_files())
        if kept:
            console.print(
                f"Kept {kept} LLM cache(s) (paid model output); "
                "use [bold]scholar cache clear --include-llm[/bold] "
                "to delete them too."
            )

    elif action == "info":
        stats = get_cache_stats()
        table = Table(title="Cache Statistics", box=box.HORIZONTALS)
        table.add_column("Metric", style="cyan")
        table.add_column("Value", style="green")

        table.add_row("Location", stats["cache_dir"])
        table.add_row("Total entries", str(stats["total_entries"]))
        size_kb = stats["total_size_bytes"] / 1024
        table.add_row("Total size", f"{size_kb:.1f} KB")
        llm_caches = stats["llm_caches"]
        if llm_caches:
            table.add_row(
                "LLM caches",
                f"{len(llm_caches)} file(s), "
                f"{sum(llm_caches.values())} entries "
                "(kept by clear without --include-llm)",
            )
        unmerged = stats["unmerged_files"]
        if unmerged:
            table.add_row("Unmerged files", f"{len(unmerged)} file(s), not used")

        console.print(table)
        <<explain the unmerged files>>

        if stats["providers"]:
            console.print()
            provider_table = Table(title="Entries per Provider", box=box.HORIZONTALS)
            provider_table.add_column("Provider", style="cyan")
            provider_table.add_column("Cached queries", justify="right")

            for provider_name, count in sorted(stats["providers"].items()):
                provider_table.add_row(provider_name, str(count))

            console.print(provider_table)

    elif action == "path":
        stats = get_cache_stats()
        print(stats["cache_dir"])

    else:
        console.print(f"[red]Unknown action: {escape(action)}[/red]")
        console.print("Valid actions: clear, info, path")
        raise typer.Exit(1)
@

\subsection{Files beside unreadable caches}

When a cache file cannot be read, a save keeps it and writes its own
entries to a file beside it instead (\cref{sec:cache-concurrency}).
Such a file is not used, and the warning that announced it is easily
missed, so [[info]] counts these files and then names them, with what
they are and what to do.
The real problem is the unreadable cache file they sit beside: as long
as it stays, every process that adds to that cache writes another such
file.
A [[clear]] deletes both, by the same rules as the caches
(\cref{sec:cache-unmerged-files}).

<<explain the unmerged files>>=
if unmerged:
    console.print()
    console.print(
        "Unmerged files hold entries that a process could not add to "
        "a cache file it could not read; the cache file was kept as it "
        "was. Check that file (the warning named it): while it stays "
        "unreadable the cache is not used. "
        "[bold]scholar cache clear[/bold] deletes both."
    )
    for path in unmerged:
        console.print(f"  {escape(path)}", soft_wrap=True)
@

\subsection{Testing the cache command}

We verify that the cache management command handles its various actions
correctly:

<<test functions>>=
