\subsection{Exporting and clearing}

[[notes export]] writes its file atomically (\cref{notes-module}), so a
failed write leaves an earlier export of the same name complete.  We
say so in one line instead of a traceback, since that earlier export is
probably the user's backup and they will want to know it survived.

<<notes command>>=
@notes_app.command("export")
def notes_export(
    output: Annotated[
        Path,
        typer.Argument(help="Output file path (JSON format)."),
    ] = Path("scholar_notes.json"),
) -> None:
    """
    Export all notes to a JSON file.

    Creates a backup of all paper notes that can be imported later.
    """
    from scholar.notes import export_notes

    console = Console()
    try:
        count = export_notes(output)
    except OSError as error:
        console.print(
            f"[red]Cannot write {escape(str(output))}: "
            f"{escape(str(error))}[/red]\n"
            "An earlier file of that name, if any, is unchanged."
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)
    console.print(
        f"[green]Exported {count} note(s) to {escape(str(output))}[/green]"
    )
@

The test makes the write fail and checks the line and the earlier
export, which must be byte-identical:

<<test functions>>=
def test_failed_notes_export_is_one_line(tmp_path, monkeypatch):
    """notes export reports a failed write and keeps the old file."""
    import scholar.notes as notes

    monkeypatch.setenv("SCHOLAR_DATA_DIR", str(tmp_path))
    output = tmp_path / "export.json"
    output.write_text("previous export")

    def failing_write(*args, **kwargs):
        raise OSError("No space left on device")

    monkeypatch.setattr(notes, "atomic_write_text", failing_write)
    result = CliRunner().invoke(app, ["notes", "export", str(output)])
    assert result.exit_code == 1
    output_text = " ".join(result.output.split())
    assert "No space left on device" in output_text
    assert "is unchanged" in output_text
    assert output.read_text() == "previous export"
