    <<warn about sessions without decisions>>
@

A session with search records but no decisions and no research context is
either not screened yet or has lost its screening, as two sessions did in
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/107}
([[needs_check]], \cref{sec:session-integrity}).  We name such sessions
in a warning after the table rather than in a column: the table and the
JSON on stdout keep their shape for scripts (the JSON gains the
[[needs_check]] field), and the warning goes to stderr, where it reaches
the user in both formats.

The Name column shows what the user types to open a session, and a
name alone is not always that: the named session [[foo]] and the
unnamed session of the query [[foo]] are both called \enquote{foo}, and
a copy holds the name of another file (\cref{sec:cli-session-arguments}).
So when the argument that opens the session differs from its name, the
column adds it in parentheses, [[foo (foo.json)]]; every other row is
unchanged.

<<sessions command>>=
def session_list_name(meta: dict) -> str:
    """The Name cell of `sessions list`: the name, and the argument if not it."""
    name = str(meta["name"])
    argument = meta.get("argument") or name
    return name if argument == name else f"{name} ({argument})"
@

<<warn about sessions without decisions>>=
flagged = [str(s["name"]) for s in sessions if s.get("needs_check")]
if flagged:
    Console(stderr=True).print(
        f"[yellow]Warning:[/yellow] {len(flagged)} session(s) have search "
        f"records but no decisions and no research context: "
        f"{escape(', '.join(flagged))}. If they were screened before, "
        "their decisions were lost; restore from <name>.json.bak in "
        "the sessions directory or from an export."
    )
<<warn about sessions stored under another name>>
@

A session file copied or renamed by hand still holds its old name
([[name_mismatch]], \cref{sec:session-own-file}).  The table shows that
stored name, but typing it opens the file that name maps to, which is
another session or none.  So we name the file beside the stored name,
and the file name is what opens the session.

<<warn about sessions stored under another name>>=
