\paragraph{Session arguments.}
\label{sec:cli-session-arguments}
Every command that takes a saved session as an argument opens it with
[[open_session_argument]], which applies the review module's rule
(\cref{sec:session-arguments}): a file name ([[foo.json]]) picks that
file, a name or query opens the one file it fits, and an argument that
fits two files (a named session [[foo]] beside the unnamed session of
the query [[foo]]) is refused with exit status~2 and a message naming
both files, before anything is read or written
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/237}); so is a path to
a file outside the sessions directory.  The commands
keep their own message and status for a session that is not found, so
the helper returns [[None]] then, like [[load_session]].  It calls the
review module's function through the module, so that a test can
replace [[review.load_session_argument]] by a stub.

<<session error helpers>>=
def open_session_argument(argument: str, *, strict: bool = False):
    """Load the session a user-typed argument names, or None if none.

    An argument that fits two session files, or a path outside the
    sessions directory, is reported on stderr and ends the command with
    exit status 2.
    """
    from scholar import review

    try:
        return review.load_session_argument(argument, strict=strict)
    except review.SessionArgumentError as error:
        Console(stderr=True).print(f"[red]{escape(str(error))}[/red]")
        raise typer.Exit(2)
