\paragraph{The [[sessions show]] command}

The [[sessions show]] command displays detailed information about a specific
review session. We always display the query used for each provider---together
with any filters and limit, reconstructed as the CLI flags that produced
them---since different providers may receive different queries (for example,
when using [[scholar rq]] with provider-specific query optimization). Before
listing papers, we show a summary of themes (tags from kept papers) with counts,
giving users a quick overview of what topics the session covers. Each paper
also shows which provider returned it, helping users understand where results
originated.

For output, we define a helper function [[print_session_details]] that prints
directly to a Rich console. This avoids the color encoding issues that arise
from rendering to a string buffer with ANSI codes and then re-printing---Rich
handles the terminal detection and styling correctly when printing directly.

Every paper is printed with its identifier on a second, dimmed line.
[[sessions decide]] addresses papers by [[--doi]] or [[--paper-id]], and
[[show]] is the command a person (or an agent) reads before deciding, so it
has to be possible to go from the listing to a decision without a detour
through a CSV export (which was the only place the DOI used to appear).
We print [[Paper.id]] rather than the bare DOI: it is exactly the string
[[--paper-id]] accepts, it exists for every paper (a DOI-less paper gets a
[[hash:]] id), and for DOI papers the DOI is readable inside it.
The three decision groups print the same line, so one helper builds it;
only the tags differ, and a pending paper simply has none.

The listing also says who decided (issue~\#163,
\cref{sec:review-source}).  Above the paper lists, \enquote{Decided by:}
counts the decisions by source, and each paper whose decision an agent
or an LLM made without a human reviewing it carries that label after its
title.  [[show]] is what an author reads before exporting, so it must
not present a list of machine verdicts as a screened list.  The JSON
form gives each decision its stored [[source]] and the counts under
[[decision_sources]], keyed by the stored values, so a script can check
what still needs review.  Each source that claims a human also says how
many of its decisions carry a record of the review
(\cref{sec:human-review-record} in the review module): \enquote{human:
1200 (0 with a record of the review)}.  The listing gives that note
always, unlike the report, because it is the working view in which a
reviewer checks what the file can show.  A decision without a record is
not thereby a machine's: decisions made before the record existed have
none either.  The JSON form gives each decision the three fields
([[human_decided_at]], [[human_decided_via]], [[human_by]], [[null]]
when unset) and the counts under [[review_records]], keyed by the
stored values of the three sources.  A decision whose source was corrected after
the fact ([[sessions relabel]], \cref{sec:sessions-relabel}) says so
after its title, naming the source the last correction replaced, and
its JSON entry carries the [[relabelled]] records (only when there are
any): a reader who sees \enquote{agent, unreviewed} should also learn
that the file once said otherwise.  The last record is the one that
explains the current source.  When the source differs from what that
record set, the mark says so too ([[relabelled from llm to agent, now
llm]]): a human's later review changes it legitimately, but so does a
process that kept its own source over a concurrent relabel
(\cref{sec:relabel-source} in the review module), and the reader
should not take the record for the current state.

A paper set back to pending ([[sessions reset]],
\cref{sec:sessions-reset}) says so after its title as well, from its
last reset record: [[reset from discarded by llm]] names the decision
it had and who made it, and its JSON entry carries the [[resets]]
records (only when there are any).  A pending paper looks like one
nobody has screened, and a reader deciding it again should know that a
screen did decide it once and what it said; the mark stays when the
paper is decided anew, since the new decision redoes an earlier one.
A relabel record that is older than the last reset described the
source of the decision the reset removed, so its mark is left out then:
next to a pending [[human]] paper it would read as a stale save
(\enquote{relabelled from llm to agent, now human}).  On a paper still
pending after a reset every relabel is older, since only a decision
can be relabelled; the times are compared only for a paper decided
anew, where they may fall in one second.  Both histories are still in
the JSON entry.

\paragraph{An abstract given by hand.}
\label{sec:sessions-show-abstract}
A paper whose abstract a screener found by hand and stored with
[[sessions set-abstract]] (\cref{sec:sessions-set-abstract}) says so
after its title, from its last record: [[abstract added by hand, from
Crossref, 2026-11-20]].  The abstract reads like any provider's, and
the next screener or classifier deciding on it should know that it
came from outside the searched databases, and from where.  When the
paper's abstract has been replaced since, by a preprint adoption
([[hand_abstract_replaced]], \cref{sec:set-abstract} in the review
module), the mark adds [[since replaced]], so that it does not vouch
for a text it no longer describes.  The JSON entry carries the
[[abstract_set]] records whole, texts included (only when there are
any): the text is part of the record, the one copy of a hand text that
a fold or an adoption replaced, and abstracts given by hand are few.
The mark itself stays one short line.

\paragraph{A classifier trace under someone else's decision.}
\label{sec:sessions-show-trace}
The classifier's fields ([[llm_model]], [[llm_reason]], \dots,
\cref{sec:llm-audit-fields} in the review module) can outlive the
classifier's decision: [[sessions relabel]] keeps them when it turns a
confirmed call into an agent's decision, [[sessions decide
--keep-trace]] keeps them under an agent's correction
(\cref{sec:sessions-decide}), and a human's decision never clears
them.  A reader who sees an agent's discard next to a model's reason
would take the reason for the agent's.  Nothing is stored to say
which call the fields describe, and nothing needs to be: when a
model is recorded but the source is not a classifier's, the fields
describe an earlier call.  The listing says so after the title
([[llm trace: earlier classifier call]]) and the JSON entry carries
[["llm_trace": "earlier classifier call"]], only for such decisions.
A decision whose source is [[llm]] or [[llm_reviewed]] is not marked:
its fields describe the call that made it, which is why
[[sessions decide]] refuses [[--keep-trace]] for a relayed classifier
verdict (\cref{sec:sessions-decide}).

The two classifier sources are named here rather than taken from the
LLM review module's [[LLM_SOURCES]]: importing that module loads the
synthesis caches, which [[sessions show]] has no use for.  A test keeps
the two sets equal.

<<sessions command>>=
EARLIER_TRACE_LABEL = "earlier classifier call"
CLASSIFIER_SOURCES = frozenset({"llm", "llm_reviewed"})


def earlier_classifier_trace(decision) -> bool:
    """Tell whether the classifier fields of `decision` describe an
    earlier call: a model is recorded, but the current source is not a
    classifier's (a relabel, an agent's correction, a human's decision).
    """
    return (
        decision.llm_model is not None
        and decision.source.value not in CLASSIFIER_SOURCES
    )
