Each line under \enquote{Queries by provider:} also says what the search
returned, as recorded in the session (\cref{sec:search-result-error}):
the hit count, or a highlighted \enquote{failed} with the reason.  This
listing is where someone auditing a review reads the query--provider
grid, so it is where a dead provider has to stand out.  Records from
before outcomes were stored show nothing, since nothing is known.
A semantic search's record shows its ranked list as one
(\enquote{top 50, ranked (not a count)}, \cref{sec:ranked-list}) and
without the \enquote{hits:} prefix, which would read as a count.  A merged record from before searches were recorded per provider
reads as one and drops the prefix for the same reason
(\cref{sec:merged-records} in the review module).
The line ends with when the search ran and, for an answer served from
the cache, when that answer was fetched---or that the cache does not
know (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/167}); the
session's own date above is only that of its last change.  The
JSON form marks such a record with [["ranked": true]] (and leaves the
key out otherwise), so a script reading [[hits]] can tell the two
apart as well.
A search that a provider sent to another endpoint than its default
names it after the provider, \enquote{[[s2 (via bulk)]]} or
\enquote{[[s2 (via bulk, most cited first)]]}
(\cref{sec:search-result-via} in the scholar module), and the JSON
form carries [[via]] for it: the bulk search stems its terms and
matches title and abstract only, which a reader comparing the counts
of a row needs to know.

