\paragraph{The works the text cites.}
\label{sec:export-cited-from}
[[--cited-from refs.bib]] (repeatable) makes the audit table's
\enquote{cited} mean what the text cites rather than what a human kept
(\cref{sec:audit-cited-from} in the review module,
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/218}).  It applies to
the audit table only: the report and the CSV list decisions, not
citations, and [[-f all]] does not include the table.  Silently
ignoring it there, as the other table options are, would let an author
believe a report followed the text when it did not, so another format
is refused before the session is read.

<<refuse [[--cited-from]] without the audit table>>=
if cited_from and format_type != "table":
    Console(stderr=True).print(
        "[red]--cited-from applies to the audit table only "
        "(-f table).[/red]"
    )
    raise typer.Exit(2)
@

The entries are not read with [[prov import]]'s reader
([[_parse_bib_entries]], \cref{sec:prov-roundtrip}).  That reader is
line-based on purpose, since [[prov export]] rewrites the file around
the line spans it records, and it takes one field per line, as Scholar
writes them.  The bibliographies a chapter cites are hand-kept, and an
editor's BibTeX mode fills long values over several lines, and may
write years as bare numbers ([[Year = 1999]]), which the line reader
does not take either.  On the intropy book's [[ltnotes.bib]] (529
entries, written that way) the line reader misses the title of 322
entries and the authors of 175; the reader below finds title and
authors in all 529, and a year in 512 (the other 17 are [[@online]]
entries without one).  An
entry whose title it missed was skipped as having \enquote{no title or
DOI}, one whose authors it missed could not match on title and first
author and was reported as not held, and one without a year turned the
namesake guard off.  So [[--cited-from]] reads the files with
bibtexparser~2, a dependency already, which parses BibTeX's own syntax
(wrapped and bare values, any case of field names), and three of its
middlewares: [[LatexDecodingMiddleware]] turns braces, accents and
macros such as [[\textquotedbl{}]] into the text the providers store,
and its name functions split the author list at [[and]] and each name
into given names, von part and surname.  They are called directly
rather than as the [[SeparateCoAuthors]] and [[SplitNameParts]]
middlewares, which act only on a field spelled [[author]], so an
[[Author]] field, as that editor writes it, would be passed over.  The
DOI is read as written, from the undecoded entry: decoding is for
prose, and it turns the [[--]] of a DOI such as [[10.18260/1-2--6093]]
into an en dash, which matched nothing;
six works of a real session's own bibliography were lost that way
before the DOI was kept apart.  A
name the functions cannot split is kept as written.  Blocks
bibtexparser cannot parse are counted on standard error.

The name parts settle the surname of several words
(\cref{sec:audit-cited-entries} in the review module).  An entry is
read twice when that matters: with its authors given name first and
surname first, [[Lopez Gallucci, Natacha]], since a session may hold
either form and only the second keeps a surname of several words
whole; the matcher tries both.  The year comes from [[year]], else
from the start of [[date]] ([[date = {2024-05-01}]]), so an entry
with [[year = {n.d.}]] still
has one for the namesake guard.  The [[edition]] field is handed over
as written, white space collapsed: the second chance of the matcher
compares it with an edition the session's title names (\enquote{3rd
edition}), and reads it there, so that [[3]], [[3rd]] and
[[third]] are one edition.  An entry with neither a title nor a
DOI cannot match a work and is skipped, by key, on standard error.  An
entry that two files share (same key and work) is matched once; two
works under one key in two files are two entries.  The summary line
counts the matches by DOI and by title, so the author sees how much of
the match rests on the weaker key, and the matches by the title without
edition or chapter number apart, as the weakest.

<<match the works the text cites>>=
cited_entries = None
cited_works = None
if cited_from:
    cited_entries = _match_cited_bibs(cited_from, session)
    cited_works = {work for entry in cited_entries for work in entry.works}
<<sessions command>>=
def _bib_value(entry, name: str):
    """The value of field `name` of a bibtexparser entry, in any case."""
    for key, field in entry.fields_dict.items():
        if key.lower() == name:
            return field.value
    return None


def _bib_year(entry) -> int | None:
    """The entry's year: from `year`, else from the start of `date`."""
    for name in ("year", "date"):
        match = re.match(r"\s*(\d{4})", str(_bib_value(entry, name) or ""))
        if match:
            return int(match.group(1))
    return None


def _bib_author_forms(entry) -> tuple[list[str], list[str]]:
    """The entry's authors given name first, and surname first."""
    from bibtexparser.middlewares.names import (
        InvalidNameError, parse_single_name_into_parts,
        split_multiple_persons_names,
    )

    given_first, surname_first = [], []
    for written in split_multiple_persons_names(
        str(_bib_value(entry, "author") or "")
    ):
        written = " ".join(written.split())
        try:
            name = parse_single_name_into_parts(written)
        except InvalidNameError:
            given_first.append(written)
            surname_first.append(written)
            continue
        surname = " ".join(name.von + name.last)
        given = " ".join(name.first)
        given_first.append(" ".join(name.first + name.von + name.last
                                    + name.jr))
        surname_first.append(f"{surname}, {given}" if given else surname)
    return given_first, surname_first


def _cited_bib_papers(paths: list[Path]) -> tuple[list, list[str], int]:
    """Read the `.bib` files as `(key, readings, edition)` tuples.

    `readings` are the entry's papers: authors given name first, and
    surname first when that names another surname; `edition` is the
    entry's `edition` field as written, or None.  Also returns the
    keys of entries with neither title nor DOI, which are skipped, and
    the number of blocks that could not be parsed.  An entry with the
    same key and work in two files is read once.
    """
    from dataclasses import replace

    import bibtexparser
    from bibtexparser import middlewares
    from scholar.review import audit_work_key
    from scholar.scholar import Paper

    entries: list = []
    skipped: list[str] = []
    failed = 0
    seen: set = set()
    for path in paths:
        try:
            text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as error:
            Console(stderr=True).print(
                f"[red]Cannot read {escape(str(path))}: "
                f"{escape(str(error))}[/red]"
            )
            raise typer.Exit(1)
        raw = bibtexparser.parse_string(text)
        decoded = middlewares.LatexDecodingMiddleware(
            allow_inplace_modification=False
        ).transform(raw)
        failed += len(raw.failed_blocks)
        for entry, written in zip(decoded.entries, raw.entries):
            title = " ".join(str(_bib_value(entry, "title") or "").split())
            doi = str(_bib_value(written, "doi") or "").strip() or None
            if not title and not doi:
                skipped.append(entry.key)
                continue
            given_first, surname_first = _bib_author_forms(entry)
            paper = Paper(title=title, authors=given_first,
                          year=_bib_year(entry), doi=doi)
            if (entry.key, paper.id) in seen:
                continue
            seen.add((entry.key, paper.id))
            readings = [paper]
            as_written = replace(paper, authors=surname_first)
            if audit_work_key(as_written) != audit_work_key(paper):
                readings.append(as_written)
            edition = " ".join(str(_bib_value(entry, "edition") or "").split())
            entries.append((entry.key, readings, edition or None))
    return entries, skipped, failed


def _match_cited_bibs(paths: list[Path], session) -> list:
    """Match the `.bib` entries to the session's works; report the count."""
    from scholar.review import (
        CHECK_BY_DOI, CHECK_BY_LOOSE_TITLE, match_cited_entries,
    )

    entries, skipped, failed = _cited_bib_papers(paths)
    cited = match_cited_entries(entries, session)
    by_doi = sum(1 for e in cited if e.matched_by == CHECK_BY_DOI)
    loose = [e for e in cited if e.matched_by == CHECK_BY_LOOSE_TITLE]
    by_title = sum(
        1 for e in cited
        if e.matched_by not in (None, CHECK_BY_DOI, CHECK_BY_LOOSE_TITLE)
    )
    by_loose = f", {len(loose)} by {CHECK_BY_LOOSE_TITLE}" if loose else ""
    unmatched = len(cited) - by_doi - by_title - len(loose)
    err = Console(stderr=True)
    err.print(
        f"Cited in {escape(', '.join(p.name for p in paths))}: "
        f"{len(cited)} entries, {by_doi} matched by DOI, {by_title} by "
        f"title{by_loose}, {unmatched} not in the session."
    )
    <<list the entries matched on a loose title>>
    if skipped:
        err.print(
            f"Skipped {len(skipped)} entries without title or DOI: "
            f"{escape(', '.join(skipped))}"
        )
    if failed:
        err.print(
            f"[yellow]Could not parse {failed} blocks of the .bib "
            "files; their entries are not matched.[/yellow]"
        )
    return cited
@

An entry found only once edition and chapter number were set aside
(\cref{sec:audit-cited-entries} in the review module, issue~\#272) is
counted under its own kind, and listed with the session title it
matched, so that the author sees each loosened match and can tell a
wrong one at a glance.  An entry whose loosened title fitted several
works was matched to none of them; it is listed too, with those works'
titles, since it also appears among the works the session does not
hold and the author should know why.  The kind is added to the count
line only when some entry was matched that way, and nothing is listed
otherwise, so a bibliography whose entries all match by DOI or work
key prints exactly what it printed before.

