After the export, the entries where the text and the screening
disagree are listed on standard error, one line per entry with its key
and a shortened title, the excluded works first: those are the ones
the table shows and the author must settle, the others have no row.

<<sessions command>>=
CITED_TITLE_LENGTH = 60


def _warn_cited_disagreements(
    session, entries: list, *, theme_names: dict[str, str], lang: str,
) -> None:
    """List the cited works the session excludes or does not hold."""
    from scholar.review import (
        AUDIT_STRINGS, cited_text_disagreements, shorten_title,
    )

    excluded, not_held = cited_text_disagreements(
        session, entries, theme_names=theme_names,
        strings=AUDIT_STRINGS[lang],
    )
    err = Console(stderr=True)
    for entries_, what in ((excluded, "the session excludes"),
                           (not_held, "the session does not hold")):
        if not entries_:
            continue
        works = "work" if len(entries_) == 1 else "works"
        err.print(
            f"[yellow]Warning: the text cites {len(entries_)} {works} "
            f"that {what}:[/yellow]"
        )
        for entry in entries_:
            title = shorten_title(entry.title, CITED_TITLE_LENGTH)
            err.print(f"  {escape(entry.key)}: {escape(title)}")
@

The command is checked end to end with two [[.bib]] files built in the
test's directory: one cites an agent's keep by DOI and a human's
discard, the other a human's keep by its title (with a [[date]] field),
a work the session lacks, a namesake ten years older than the session's
work, which only its [[date]] tells apart, the first file's DOI entry
again, and an entry with neither title nor DOI.  A further session
work is cited by a surname of two words.  Without the option the
export must be exactly what the audit table generator writes without
it, which has no rule sentence; and the option is refused for another
format, [[all]] included.  Four more cases come from the reader: an
entry in the editor's layout (tab-filled, title and authors wrapped,
bare years, LaTeX accents and [[\textquotedbl{}]]) must match by
title, and its namesake a decade later must not; a [[year = {n.d.}]]
takes its year from [[date]]; one key for two works in two files
is two entries; and a block bibtexparser refuses, here a second entry
under one key in one file, is counted while the others still match.

