A machine decision must not replace a human one silently.  Recording an
agent's verdict on a paper the author had kept under a theme used to
demote that decision to an unreviewed one and, on a discard, turn the
author's themes into the agent's motivations; nothing said it had
happened.  So before anything changes, every selected paper that a
human decided, or whose machine decision a human reviewed, is a
conflict when the new source is a machine.  Conflicts are handled like
unmatched selectors: all of them are named, with the decision that
stands, nothing is saved, and the command exits with status~2, the
usage-error status of the missing [[--source]].  [[--force]] is the
deliberate override.  An unknown stored source does not count as a
human decision here (\cref{sec:review-source}), and a pending paper
has no decision to protect, though its default source is [[human]].

<<refuse to let a machine replace a human decision>>=
if source in MACHINE_DECISION_SOURCES and not force:
    conflicts = [
        decision for decision in selected.values()
        if decision.is_decided and decision.source in (
            ReviewSource.HUMAN, ReviewSource.LLM_REVIEWED,
            ReviewSource.AGENT_REVIEWED,
        )
    ]
    for decision in conflicts:
        console.print(
            f"[red]{escape(decision.paper.id)} is already "
            f"{decision.status.value} ({decision.source.value}); an "
            f"{source} decision would replace it.[/red]"
        )
    if conflicts:
        console.print(
            "[yellow]No changes saved. Pass --force to replace a human "
            "decision.[/yellow]"
        )
        raise typer.Exit(2)
@

<<sessions command>>=
MACHINE_DECISION_SOURCES = ("agent", "llm")
@

Tags used to be only added, whatever happened to the status
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/177}). A paper the LLM
had kept as [[supports-claim]] and a reviewer then discarded with
[[--tag no-evidence]] carried both tags, and the audit table, which
reads the category from the tags, listed it as excluded although
classified as bearing on the claim: the old classification survived
the decision that overturned it. When the status changes, the new
decision therefore removes the old category tags, and so does a
decision that brings a category of its own even though the status
stands (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/195}): an agent
that re-decides [[--keep -t qualifies-claim]] on a record kept as
[[supports-claim]] used to leave both, and the audit table's last-wins
rule hid that the record held two classifications
([[tags_after_decision]], \cref{sec:audit-table}, which explains the
rule and why nothing else is removed). A same-status decision without a category
refines the record and the tags accumulate. [[add_tag]] is idempotent,
so a repeated tag is not doubled.

Nothing else removes a tag, and an exclusion criterion such as
[[no-abstract]] left on a record that was discarded and then kept reads
as a theme, which makes the audit table count the kept row as cited for
that reason. A decision names at most one category: two among the
[[--tag]]s would store both, which is the state this rule avoids, so
that is a usage error (status~2, checked with the other options before
the session is loaded) naming them. [[--untag]] removes named tags from every selected record,
before [[--tag]] adds, so [[--untag no-abstract --tag ml]] replaces one
by the other. A machine source may untag as it may tag; the guard
against replacing a human's decision (above) is unchanged, since it runs
before any tag is touched. A tag that no selected record held is not an
error, since a script run twice should not fail the second time, but it
is named on stderr, once per tag, because a misspelt tag would otherwise
look like a success. [[--untag]] is part of a decision, so it needs
[[--keep]] or [[--discard]] and has their other effects: a machine
