An agent that records a classifier's verdicts through [[decide]] also
has the classifier's confidence, and the audit table promises one for
every machine-classified row (issue~\#90). [[--confidence]] stores it
where [[scholar llm classify]] does, in [[llm_confidence]], so the two
paths produce the same row; an agent deciding by itself can state its
own confidence the same way. It belongs to the machine, so it is stored
only with [[--source agent]] or [[llm]]: a human decision on a machine
row is a review,
and overwriting the model's number with the reviewer's would make the
table misreport what the model said, while a plain human decision has
no machine label to qualify. Given with a human source the option is
therefore accepted but ignored, with a note, rather than refused, so a
script that always passes it keeps working. Without the option a
machine decision has no confidence, and the table marks it as missing:
a number left from an earlier classifier run would be shown as the
confidence of a decision that run did not make. Like the other checks,
the range is validated before the session is loaded.

