\paragraph{Keeping the classifier's trace.}
A machine decision clears the fields an earlier classifier run stored
(above, in [[<<record one decision with its source>>]]), and by
default that is right: a new verdict replaces the old one, and the
old run's model, prompt and reason would describe a decision that no
longer stands.  One case wants the opposite
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/245}).  After
[[sessions relabel]] has turned classifier calls that an agent
confirmed into [[agent]] decisions (\cref{sec:sessions-relabel}),
some of them still need a correction, such as a discarded record that
carries the category [[supports-claim]].  The agent records the
correction as its own verdict, but the classifier call it corrects is
still part of the record: clearing it would drop the model and the
prompt from the report's LLM screening section, which counts the
decisions an LLM made first ([[first_made_by_llm]] in the LLM review
module).  [[--keep-trace]] asks for that: the agent's decision is
recorded as usual (status, tags with the category replacement and
[[--untag]], source, [[is_example]] cleared, the guard against
replacing a human decision and [[--force]], [[--all-copies]]), only
[[clear_machine_audit]] is not called.  Without the flag nothing
changes.

The flag is for [[--source agent]] only.  A verdict relayed with
[[--source llm]] is a new classifier run, and its decision would carry
the old run's model, prompt and reason, which every reader (the
report's LLM screening section, [[sessions show]]) takes for the
run that made it: nothing on the record could tell the two apart.
So that combination is a usage error (status~2), refused with the
other options before the session is loaded.

The flag needs [[--confidence]].  The confidence is not one of the
classifier's fields: it belongs to whoever made the current decision,
and the audit table prints it as that decider's number.  A machine
decision without [[-c]] clears it (above), so a corrector that left
it out would get the table's \enquote{missing} mark; but if the flag
kept it, the classifier's number would be shown as the agent's
without anybody having said so.  A correction on top of a kept trace
therefore states its own number, and leaving it out is a usage error
(status~2), refused with the other options before the session is
loaded.  A human decision never clears the trace
(\cref{sec:llm-audit-fields} in the review module), so for a human
the flag has nothing to do: it is accepted with a note, as
[[--confidence]] is, so that a script that always passes it keeps
working, and it does not ask for [[-c]], which a human decision
ignores.

