\paragraph{The agent's reason.}
An agent that screens with [[--source agent]] used to leave its
verdict and nothing that said why
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/249}), while
[[scholar llm classify]] stores the model's reason for every record.
[[--reason TEXT]] stores the agent's, with [[--by NAME]] and the time
of the run, in the decision's agent trace
(\cref{sec:agent-audit-fields} in the review module).  [[--by]] is
whatever the agent calls itself; left out, it stays empty, because a
login or a host name guessed here would be an author's name on a
record that an agent wrote.  One time stamp serves the whole run: the
decisions of one command were made together, and a time per record
would only measure how long the loop took.

The options are for [[--source agent]] only.  A relayed classifier
verdict ([[--source llm]]) has its reason recorded by the classifier,
in [[llm_reason]]; an agent's reason under a classifier's source would
be read as the model's.  A human's reason is out of scope: the fields
are the agent's trace, which a human's later review is compared with,
and a human's own words would overwrite what is to be compared.  Both
are usage errors (status~2), refused before the session is loaded, and
the message says which of the two applies.  So is [[--by]] without a
reason for an agent: a name with nothing to attach it to is not a
trace.  For a human, [[--by]] is accepted alone and names who decided
in the human's review record (below); with [[--source llm]] it is
refused like the reasons.  [[--strict-quotes]] without a reason is
refused too, since it would have no quote to check
(\cref{sec:decide-quote-check}).

Most screens decide many records with one verdict but a reason of
their own each.  One command per record would work, but each command
loads and saves the whole session, which for 30\,000 records is the
slow part.  [[--reasons-file FILE]] gives each line its own reason:
\begin{verbatim}
doi:10.1145/3568813<TAB>Studies novices' misconceptions of recursion.
10.1145/3568814<TAB>Teaching material, no evaluation.<TAB>0.7
hash:0123456789abcdef<TAB>Survey of compilers, not of learners.
\end{verbatim}
The first column is the session's paper id or a DOI, with or without
[[doi:]], as [[--paper-id]] and [[--doi]] take them; the lines of the
file \emph{are} the selection, so the file cannot be combined with
[[--doi]] or [[--paper-id]] (status~2), which would leave some
selected papers without a reason.  Status, tags, [[--untag]] and [[-c]]
apply to every line, so an agent groups its calls by verdict, one file
per verdict.  The confidence is the one exception: an agent's
confidence is usually per record, so an optional third column gives a
line its own, which replaces [[-c]] for that line (a line without it
takes [[-c]], or none).  A tab inside a reason is not allowed.  It
could be split on the first two tabs, but a reason that happens to
contain a tab followed by a number would then be read as a confidence
without a word, so a fourth column is refused instead.  Blank lines
and lines starting with [[#]] are skipped, so an agent can annotate
its file.

Every check that does not need the session is made before it is
loaded, and every bad line is named, not only the first: a line
without a tab, an empty id, a reason that is empty after stripping or
longer than [[MAX_AGENT_REASON_CHARS]] (2000, see the review module
for why), a confidence that is not a number between 0 and~1 (the range
of [[-c]]), a fourth column, a file with no line at all.  Such a file
is a malformed argument, so it is a usage error (status~2) and nothing
is saved.  A reason given with [[--reason]] is checked the same way.
A reason is stripped of surrounding whitespace, since an agent that
writes a trailing newline has not said anything more, and is never
truncated.  Once the session is loaded, an id that matches no decision
aborts the batch like an unmatched [[--doi]] (status~1, every one
named), and so does one paper given two different reasons or
confidences, by repeating its line or by naming it once by id and once
by DOI (status~2): which of the two is the agent's is not ours to
guess.  The same line twice is harmless.

An agent decision without a reason is still recorded, because scripts
written before the option existed must keep working, but a one-line
warning on standard error says that it carries none.  The report counts
such decisions (\cref{sec:report-provenance} in the review module).

