[[checked_agent_reason]] strips a reason and returns it with what is
wrong with it, if anything.  The limit is the review module's, so that
the CLI and the stored field cannot disagree.

<<sessions command>>=
def checked_agent_reason(text: str) -> tuple[str, str | None]:
    """Strip an agent's reason; return it and its problem, if any."""
    from scholar.review import MAX_AGENT_REASON_CHARS

    stripped = text.strip()
    if not stripped:
        return stripped, "empty reason"
    if not any(c.isalnum() for c in stripped):
        return stripped, f"reason {stripped!r} has no letter or digit"
    if is_placeholder_reason(stripped):
        return stripped, (
            f"reason {stripped!r} is a placeholder, not a reason"
        )
    if len(stripped) > MAX_AGENT_REASON_CHARS:
        return stripped, (
            f"reason of {len(stripped)} characters, over the limit of "
            f"{MAX_AGENT_REASON_CHARS}"
        )
    return stripped, None


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class ReasonLine:
    """One line of a `--reasons-file`: which paper, why, how sure."""

    line: int
    paper: str
    reason: str
    confidence: float | None


def parse_reasons_file(text: str) -> tuple[list[ReasonLine], list[str]]:
    """Parse a `--reasons-file`: `ID<TAB>REASON[<TAB>CONFIDENCE]` lines.

    Returns the valid lines and a problem per invalid line, naming its
    number. Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are skipped.
    """
    entries, problems = parse_tab_file(text, REASONS_LAYOUT)
    return [
        ReasonLine(number, paper, reason, confidence)
        for number, paper, (reason, confidence) in entries
    ], problems
@

The rules of the lines are not the reasons file's own: [[sessions
screen]] reads a file of verdicts with the same conventions
(\cref{sec:sessions-screen}), and two parsers would drift apart in
what they accept.  So the line rules live in [[parse_tab_file]], and a
file's columns are given to it as a layout ([[TabFileLayout]]): after
the id, which every file has first, one [[TabFileColumn]] per column,
each with a function that reads its text into a value and says what is
wrong with it, and whether it may be left out.  A layout also names
what a line holds, for the messages, and may add a check of the whole
line once every column has been read.  The reasons file is the first
layout: a reason and an optional confidence.

<<sessions command>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class TabFileColumn:
    """A column after the id of a tab-separated batch file.

    `read` turns the column's text into its value and the problem with
    it (None if there is none). An `optional` column may be absent or
    empty, and then reads as None.
    """

    read: Callable[[str], tuple[Any, str | None]]
    optional: bool = False


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class TabFileLayout:
    """The columns of a batch file after its id, and how lines are named.

    `too_few` is the problem of a line with fewer columns than the
    required ones; `holds` says what a line holds and `last` names the
    last column, for a line with too many; `check`, if given, returns
    the problems of a line whose columns all read well.
    """

    columns: tuple[TabFileColumn, ...]
    too_few: str
    holds: str
    last: str
    check: Callable[[tuple], list[str]] | None = None


def parse_tab_file(
    text: str, layout: TabFileLayout
) -> tuple[list[tuple[int, str, tuple]], list[str]]:
    """Parse the lines of a tab-separated batch file by `layout`.

    Returns `(line number, id, column values)` for each valid line and
    a problem per invalid line, naming its number. Blank lines and lines
    starting with `#` are skipped.
    """
    entries: list[tuple[int, str, tuple]] = []
    problems: list[str] = []
    for number, raw in enumerate(text.split("\n"), start=1):
        raw = raw.removesuffix("\r")
        if not raw.strip() or raw.lstrip().startswith("#"):
            continue
        <<parse one line of a batch file>>
    return entries, problems
