Only a newline ends a line, and a carriage return before it is dropped
(a file saved on Windows).  [[str.splitlines]] would also split at a
lone carriage return, a line separator (U+2028), a next-line
character, a form feed and other
characters that a reason copied from a PDF may hold: the reason would
be cut in two, the second half read as a line without a tab, and every
error after it would name a line number one higher than the editor
shows.  Splitting at newlines alone keeps such characters inside the
reason, where they are stored as they were written.

A line holds the id, the required columns and, after them, the
optional ones.  An empty optional column (a spreadsheet's export of an
empty cell) counts as absent rather than as a bad value.  A column more
than the layout has comes from a tab inside the reason or from tabs
after the last column; the message names each extra column and what it
holds, since an empty one (two trailing tabs) is otherwise invisible.
The file is not quoted, as a CSV file would be: quote marks are part
of the reason and stored as written.  The problems of one line are
joined in the order of its columns, the empty id first.

