@

Selecting every copy raises the question what a decision does to two
copies decided apart, one discarded and one kept, say.  Giving both the
new decision settles the disagreement that the copy lists show, but
also erases it: whoever named the id need not know there were two
copies, a script or an agent least of all, and the other copy's verdict
and motivation would be gone without a word.  So a batch that selects
such copies ([[same_id_pairs]], the pairs the lists show as conflicting
or alike) is refused, unsaved, with exit code 2 and each pair named as
the lists name it, like the refusal of a machine decision over a human
one below.  [[--all-copies]] says that the decision is meant for every
copy, which is how a user who has seen the pair settles it.  Copies
that already agree, or a pending copy beside a decided one, have no
disagreement to lose and are decided together without the flag.

<<refuse to decide copies decided apart>>=
if not all_copies:
    apart = [
        pair for pair in same_id_pairs(session)
        if any(id(d) in selected for d in pair)
    ]
    for pair in apart:
        console.print(
            f"[red]{escape(copy_conflict_line(CopyConflict(list(pair))))}"
            "[/red]",
            soft_wrap=True,
        )
    if apart:
        console.print(
            "[red]These copies of one work are decided apart; pass "
            "--all-copies to give every copy this decision.[/red]\n"
            "[yellow]No changes saved.[/yellow]"
        )
        raise typer.Exit(2)
