\subsection{Recording a second screener's verdicts}
\label{sec:sessions-screen}

[[sessions screen]] records another screener's verdicts of papers in a
session as screenings (\cref{sec:screenings} in the review module),
beside the decisions, which it leaves as they are
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/253}).  It is a
command of its own rather than an option of [[sessions decide]],
because [[decide]] means \enquote{set the outcome}, and every guard it
has ([[--force]], [[--keep-trace]], the refusal to replace a human's
decision) is about that.  A screening sets no outcome, so none of
those guards applies, and none of their options belongs here.

Who screened is given with [[--as]]: [[agent]] for an AI agent's
verdicts; [[human]] for a second human screener who records without
the TUI; and [[llm]] for a classifier's verdicts relayed from outside
Scholar.  Every screening names its screener with [[--by]] (the model,
for a classifier), and the command refuses to run without it
(status~2, before the session is loaded).  A screening is one
screener's verdict, to be compared with another's, and a screener is
told apart by its kind and name.  Without a name the comparison would
fail in the most common case: a decision an unnamed agent made (every
agent decision from before [[--reason]] and [[--by]] existed has no
name) is snapshot as \enquote{agent, no name}, and an unnamed agent's
screening of it would be recorded under the same identity, as if one
screener had said both.  The name is taken as given, never guessed,
as for an agent's reason.  Like [[--source]] of [[sessions decide]],
[[--as]] defaults to [[human]] at a terminal and is required
otherwise (status~2), so that an agent that leaves it out is not
recorded as a human.

The papers and verdicts are given in one of two ways.  On the command
line, papers are selected with [[--doi]] and [[--paper-id]], and all
get one verdict ([[--keep]] or [[--discard]]), its [[--tag]]s, an
optional [[--reason]] and [[-c]].  A screen of many papers gives each
its own verdict in [[--verdicts-file]], one line per paper:
\begin{verbatim}
doi:10.1145/3568813<TAB>kept<TAB>supports-claim,recursion<TAB>Reports ...
10.1145/3568814<TAB>discarded<TAB>off-topic-false-hit<TAB>A compiler ...<TAB>0.9
\end{verbatim}
that is, the id, [[kept]] or [[discarded]], the tags separated by
commas (the column may be empty for a keep), the reason and an
optional confidence.  The file follows every convention of
[[--reasons-file]] (\cref{sec:sessions-decide}): read as
[[utf-8-sig]], lines ended by a newline only, blank and [[#]] lines
skipped, the id a paper id or a DOI with or without [[doi:]], no
quoting, every bad line named and refused before the session is
loaded (status~2), placeholders and shifted columns refused, and the
quotes of each reason checked against its own record once the session
is loaded, listed on standard error or, with [[--strict-quotes]],
refusing the batch.  It does so by sharing the code:
[[parse_verdicts_file]] is [[parse_tab_file]] with a layout of its
own, and the quote check is the one [[sessions decide]] runs.

A discard needs a tag, as in [[sessions decide]] and the TUI, and a
verdict holds at most one category tag.  When the session uses
categories at all (any decision has one of [[AUDIT_CATEGORIES]]), a
machine's screening, an agent's or a relayed classifier's, must carry
exactly one, fitting its status ([[STATUS_CATEGORIES]], the rule the
classifier is held to, \cref{sec:category-fits-status} in the LLM
review module).  This is stricter than [[sessions decide]], which lets
an agent record a supporting paper as excluded: a decision may
deliberately exclude a relevant hit under a rule of the review, but a
screening is there to be compared with another screener's, by status
and by category, and a category that contradicts its own status would
make one verdict disagree with itself.  A human's screening is free in
this, as a human's decision is, and one without a category is stored
as it is.  A human screening carries no confidence either: [[-c]] is
the machine's confidence wherever Scholar stores one, so with
[[--as human]] it is refused rather than dropped.

[[--blind]] records that the screener did not see the other verdicts,
[[--not-blind]] that it did, both as declared by the caller; without
either nothing is claimed.  Scholar cannot know whether an agent
looked: an agent that can run Scholar can read the session file.
[[--sample ID]] records which review sample
(\cref{sec:llm-sample-record} in the review module) the screen was
made for; it must name a sample of the session, by its id or, for a
sample drawn in confidence bands, by the id the bands share.  Whether
every screened paper was drawn in it is a question for the reports
that read the samples, not for this command.  Copies of one work
decided apart under one id ([[same_id_pairs]]) need [[--all-copies]],
for the reason given with [[sessions decide]]: a caller who named the
id need not know there were two.

The command records nothing else: the decision, its source, its trace
and the human's record are not touched, which is what the tests below
check on the saved file.  With [[--dry-run]] it prints what it would
record and writes nothing.  Otherwise it copies the session file with
[[keep_copy_before_run]] under the label [[pre-screen]] and saves with
[[save_session]], which merges what other processes saved meanwhile.
All screenings of one run share one time, [[recorded_at]], as the
decisions of one [[sessions decide]] run share theirs.

