\subsection{Checking sessions with a semantic search}
\label{sec:sessions-check}

A keyword search's record shows what was found and cannot show what was
missed (issue~\#136).  [[sessions check]] puts a search by meaning next
to it: for each question it runs the [[openalex-semantic]] provider
(\cref{sec:openalex-semantic} in the providers chapter) and looks every
returned work up in the given sessions
([[check_against_sessions]], \cref{sec:semantic-check} in the review
chapter).  It prints, per work, its place in the ranking, its bucket
(bearing, adjacent, other topic, excluded, unclassified, or not in any
session) and how each session classified it; then how many of the
works are in no session; then where each [[--known]] study ended up.

\begin{verbatim}
scholar sessions check review-a review-b \
    --semantic "How do users regain access to a lost account?" \
    --known 10.1145/3576915.3623180 -f json -o check/
\end{verbatim}

The command is strictly read-only on the sessions: the check is
evidence about the review and must not change the record it is evidence
about, so it loads each session and never saves one.  A session that
does not exist or does not parse stops the command with exit code 1
and its name, before any request, since a check against fewer sessions
than asked for would report false gaps.

It reuses the provider's [[search()]], so the cache, the pacing at one
request a second and the shared OpenAlex budget all apply, and a
question asked again costs nothing.  A question whose search failed is
reported as failed, with the reason, and the exit code is then 1: a
failed check found no gap, and a script must not read it as a clean
one.  The limit is at most fifty, the most the search returns, and
fifty by default.

[[-o]] keeps the evidence with the audit record, one pair of files per
question, numbered in the order the questions were given:
[[<n>.json]], the question with the works as returned and converted by
the provider, each with its rank and OpenAlex's [[relevance_score]],
and [[<n>.txt]], the list as printed in the table format.
The JSON output ([[-f json]]) carries, per question, the question, the
provider, the limit, the date of the check, where the answer came from
and every row, so it can be kept as it is.  The CSV has one line per
returned work and one per known study (its [[kind]] column tells
which), so a spreadsheet can sort them.

Evidence has to be dated correctly.  The provider's cache never
expires, so a question asked again may be answered from a fetch made
months before the check.  Each question therefore records two dates:
[[check_date]], when this command ran, and the answer's source, read
from the provider (\cref{sec:openalex-semantic} keeps the fetch time
with the cached answer): \enquote{fetched} with its time,
\enquote{cached, fetched} with the original time, or \enquote{cached
(date unknown)} when the cache holds no time.  The check date is never
passed off as the fetch time.  A provider that does not say (a
third-party provider, or a stand-in in a test) is reported as
\enquote{fetched (date unknown)}.

Each question also gets a tally of its buckets
([[check_tally]], \cref{sec:semantic-check}), \enquote{8 works: 1
bearing, 7 not in any session}, the one line to read first, as the
earlier script printed it.

<<sessions command>>=
@sessions_app.command("check")
def sessions_check(
    names: Annotated[
        list[str],
        typer.Argument(
            help="Sessions to look the returned works up in.",
            autocompletion=complete_session_name,
        ),
    ],
    <<sessions check options>>
) -> None:
    """
    Check sessions against a semantic search (OpenAlex search.semantic,
    or Semantic Scholar's snippet search with --backend s2-snippets).

    For each --semantic question, runs a search by meaning and prints,
    per returned work, its place, how the sessions classified it, and
    which works are in no session. A check on a keyword search, not a
    search of its own: it returns a ranked list of at most 50 works.
    The sessions are only read. Exit code 1 if a session is missing or
    a question's search failed.
    """
    <<validate the check arguments>>
    <<load the sessions to check, read-only>>
    <<run each semantic check>>
    <<print the check results>>
    <<write the check evidence files>>
    if any(result["error"] for result in results):
        raise typer.Exit(1)
@

The options follow the issue's sketch.  [[--semantic]] is repeatable,
one question per concept, and so is [[--known]].
[[--backend]] picks the search that answers: [[openalex]], the
default, is OpenAlex's semantic search, and [[s2-snippets]] is
Semantic Scholar's snippet search, which matches passages of the full
text as well as title and abstract (\cref{sec:s2-snippets} in the
providers chapter).
The output is the same for both, and the provider line of each
question, the [[provider]] field of the JSON and the evidence files
name the backend that answered ([[openalex-semantic]] or
[[s2-snippets]]).
Both are ranked lists, so each is labelled as one and never as a count
(\enquote{top 8, ranked (not a count)}).
The limit stays at fifty works for both: the snippet search could give
more, but a check is a list for a person to read, and the same length
keeps the two backends' lists comparable.
The question's length is checked only for OpenAlex, which documents
its 2\,000 characters; the snippet search documents no maximum.
The snippet backend looks up the DOI and year of its works after the
search; when that lookup fails, its works are matched by title and
first author only, and the backend's [[last_note]] says so.
The note is printed under the question's provider line and kept in the
JSON and the evidence file as [[note]] ([[None]] when there is none).

<<sessions check options>>=
semantic: Annotated[
