\section{Citation Provenance}
\label{sec:prov-commands}

When a citation is backed by a paper, its provenance record needs a
[[FOUND-VIA]] line: a command that reproduces the search which surfaced the
paper.  A [[scholar rq]] session already records, per provider, the query it
sent to each database (in [[query_provider_pairs]]).  But [[rq]] generates those
queries with a language model, so re-running [[scholar rq]] on the same research
question is \emph{not} reproducible---a later run may phrase the queries
differently and return a different set.  The artifact that \emph{is}
reproducible is the exact query that was actually sent, which the session has
already persisted.  So [[found-via]] rebuilds a [[scholar search]] command from
that stored query rather than re-deriving it through the model, and demotes the
research question to human-readable context.

The lookup runs backwards from the one piece of information the author has at
citation time---a paper ID.  There is no index from a paper back to the sessions
that contain it, so [[found-via]] scans the saved sessions and reports every one
that returned the paper (a paper may have been found in more than one search).
We report it regardless of the keep/discard decision: provenance records
\emph{how} a paper surfaced, which is independent of whether the reviewer later
kept it, and an author may want the trail for a paper still under consideration.

\subsection{The prov command group}

We expose provenance under its own [[prov]] command group, mirroring the
[[sessions]] and [[notes]] groups.  This one group hosts both the read-only
[[found-via]] export described next and the [[import]]/[[export]] round-trip
between [[.bib]] files and notes built in \cref{sec:prov-roundtrip}; defining it
here, before either set of subcommands, lets all of them attach to the same
[[prov_app]].

<<prov command>>=
prov_app = typer.Typer(
    name="prov",
    help="Export and round-trip citation provenance.",
    no_args_is_help=True,
)
app.add_typer(prov_app, name="prov")
@

\subsection{Reconstructing a reproducible search}

The record for one paper in one session pins the command to the search that
actually returned the paper: re-running every query would reproduce the
whole session, not the one result we are citing.  Each decision keeps the
[[(query, provider, filters)]] keys of the searches that returned its
paper ([[found_by]], \cref{sec:review-found-by}), and [[search_record]]
turns a key back into the stored search with its filters and limit.  The
filters are part of the key, so the same query run with two filter sets
gives two different commands, each with its own flags.  Those become
the same flags the [[bibtex+prov]] formatter emits, through the shared
[[_search_command]], so the command reproduces the \emph{exact} search and
not a broader one.  A paper that several searches returned gets a record
for each.

Sessions saved before the links existed have none, and then we must not
guess.  The provider alone used to stand in for the link, and picking that
provider's search named a query that had never returned the paper
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/128}): a FOUND-VIA line
looks verified, so a wrong one is worse than none.  There is one case the
provider settles: if the session ran a single search on it, that search
returned the paper, since [[decision.provider]] is the provider of a
search that did.  We report that as an [[inferred]] link, with the same
command.  Otherwise the link is [[missing]]: no command, and the
provider's searches listed as candidates, as queries and as commands, so
the author can see what to re-run.  Snowballed papers are always missing,
since no search returned them.

The research question travels along as [[research_context]] for the human
reader, but it never shapes the command.  We deliberately do \emph{not} key the
command on whether [[research_context]] is set: it can be filled in on any
session to drive automated classification, so it is not a marker of how the
session was built.

<<prov command>>=
def _found_via_records(session, decision) -> list[dict]:
    """Build the provenance records of one paper in one session.

    One record per search that returned the paper, each with the
    `scholar search` command that reproduces it. Without recorded links,
    a provider searched only once is still certain (`link` "inferred");
    otherwise one record with no command (`link` "missing").
    """
    base = {
        "paper_id": decision.paper.id,
        "title": decision.paper.title,
        "session": session.name or session.query,
        "research_context": session.research_context,
        "timestamp": session.timestamp.isoformat(),
    }
    <<find the searches that returned the paper>>
    records = []
    for record in searches:
        records.append({
            **base,
            "command": _search_command(
                record.query, record.provider, record.filters, record.limit
            ),
            "provider": record.provider,
            "provider_query": record.query,
            "filters": record.filters,
            "limit": record.limit,
            "link": link,
        })
    for query, provider in unresolved:
        candidates = [
            record for record in session.search_records
            if (record.query, record.provider) == (query, provider)
        ]
        records.append(_missing_link_record(base, provider, candidates))
    if not records:
        candidates = [
            record for record in session.search_records
            if record.provider == decision.provider
        ]
        records.append(
            _missing_link_record(base, decision.provider, candidates)
        )
    return records


def _missing_link_record(base: dict, provider: str, candidates) -> dict:
    """A provenance record without a command, listing the candidates."""
    return {
        **base,
        "command": None,
        "provider": provider,
        "provider_query": None,
        "filters": None,
        "limit": None,
        "link": "missing",
        "candidate_queries": [r.query for r in candidates],
        "candidate_commands": [
            _search_command(r.query, r.provider, r.filters, r.limit)
            for r in candidates
        ],
    }
@

A recorded link whose search record is gone cannot be turned into a
command; that does not happen (records are never removed), and we skip such
a link rather than fail.  An old link that names no filters and matches
several records---one query run with two filter sets
(\cref{sec:search-record-key} in the [[review]] module)---is not gone
but unresolved.  It is reported like a missing link, under
[[Not reproducible:]], with the records of its query and provider as the
candidates, each with its own filter flags; the paper's resolved links
are still printed beside it.  Choosing one of the candidates would be
the guess that \#128 showed to be wrong.  The inference compares the provider string
exactly, so a merged provider such as [[openalex, dblp]] infers only its
own merged record.

<<find the searches that returned the paper>>=
unresolved = []
if decision.found_by:
    link = "recorded"
    searches = []
    for query, provider, key in decision.found_by:
        record = session.search_record(query, provider, key)
        if record is not None:
            searches.append(record)
        elif key is None:
            unresolved.append((query, provider))
else:
    link = "inferred"
    searches = [
        record for record in session.search_records
        if record.provider == decision.provider
    ]
    if len(searches) != 1:
        searches = []
@

\subsection{The found-via command}

The command resolves a paper ID the same way [[notes show]] does---exact match,
or substring so a bare DOI matches the [[doi:]]-prefixed ID---and follows the
sub-app [[--format]] convention so tooling can ask for JSON.  We walk the
sessions newest-first (the natural reading order for provenance: the most recent
search comes first) and collect every decision whose paper matches.

Resolving by substring is convenient but can be \emph{ambiguous}: a short
fragment may match two unrelated papers, and emitting provenance for both would
silently attribute the wrong search to a citation.  So we sharpen the match
before reporting.  An exact ID always wins over looser substring hits.  Failing
that, if the fragment still spans more than one distinct paper we refuse to
guess and ask for a full ID rather than print blocks the author cannot tell
apart.

The error paths respect [[--format]] too: JSON output must always be parseable,
so the not-found and ambiguous cases emit an empty array on [[stdout]] (with the
non-zero exit signalling the failure) instead of the human-readable message a
table reader gets.

<<prov command>>=
@prov_app.command("found-via")
def prov_found_via(
    paper_id: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Argument(
            help="Paper ID (DOI or hash) to trace.",
            autocompletion=complete_session_paper_id,
        ),
    ],
    output_format: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Option(
            "--format", "-f",
            help="Output format: table or json.",
            autocompletion=complete_display_format,
        ),
    ] = "table",
) -> None:
    """
    Print a reproducible FOUND-VIA provenance line for a paper.

    Scans saved review sessions for the paper and, for each session that
    returned it, prints the 'scholar search' command that reproduces the
    search which surfaced it. Use '-f json' for machine-readable output.
    """
    from scholar.review import sessions_mentioning

    console = Console()
    <<collect matching decisions for the paper>>
    <<resolve the matches to a single paper>>
    records = [r for s, d in matches for r in _found_via_records(s, d)]

    if not records:
        if output_format == "json":
            print("[]")
        else:
            console.print(
                f"[red]No saved session contains paper ID: "
                f"{escape(paper_id)}[/red]"
            )
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    if output_format == "json":
        print(json.dumps(records, indent=2))
    else:
        <<print FOUND-VIA blocks>>
@

The scan matches every decision's paper in every session that mentions the
ID, remembering the distinct IDs hit so we can detect ambiguity afterwards.
[[sessions_mentioning]] decodes only the session files whose text contains
the ID (\cref{sec:review-sessions-mentioning} explains why that misses
none); decoding them all made one lookup take over a minute on a large data
directory.  A
session that fails to load is skipped with a warning rather than fatal, so
one corrupt file does not hide the provenance held in the others.

<<collect matching decisions for the paper>>=
matches = []  # (session, decision) pairs, newest session first
matched_ids = set()
for session in sessions_mentioning(paper_id):
    for decision in session.decisions:
        pid = decision.paper.id
        if pid == paper_id or paper_id in pid:
            matches.append((session, decision))
            matched_ids.add(pid)
@

An exact ID match collapses the set to that one paper (it may still span several
sessions, which is the legitimate multi-trail case).  Only when there is no
exact match \emph{and} the substring reached more than one paper do we treat the
query as ambiguous.

<<resolve the matches to a single paper>>=
if paper_id in matched_ids:
    matches = [(s, d) for s, d in matches if d.paper.id == paper_id]
elif len(matched_ids) > 1:
    if output_format == "json":
        print("[]")
    else:
        console.print(
            f"[red]Ambiguous paper ID '{escape(paper_id)}' matches "
            f"{len(matched_ids)} papers; re-run with a full ID:[/red]"
        )
        for pid in sorted(matched_ids):
            console.print(f"[red]  {escape(pid)}[/red]")
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

Each block prints the reproducible command on the first line and the session
plus research-question context on a second, indented to line up under the
command.  The session name and question are wrapped in double quotes for
readability, so a double quote inside either would unbalance the line; we escape
those.  We also collapse any internal whitespace in the research question so a
question entered across several lines still renders as one context line, and we
omit the question entirely when the session never recorded one.

<<print FOUND-VIA blocks>>=
blocks = []
for record in records:
    session_name = record["session"].replace('"', '\\"')
    context = f'(session "{session_name}"'
    research = record["research_context"]
    if research:
        question = " ".join(research.split()).replace('"', '\\"')
        context += f'; research question: "{question}"'
    context += ")"
    if record["command"] is not None:
        prefix, lines = FOUND_VIA_PREFIX, [record["command"]]
    else:
        prefix, lines = NO_FOUND_VIA_PREFIX, _missing_link_lines(record)
    indent = " " * len(prefix)
    blocks.append(
        prefix + f"\n{indent}".join(lines + [context])
    )
print("\n\n".join(blocks))
<<constants>>=
FOUND_VIA_PREFIX = "FOUND-VIA: "
NO_FOUND_VIA_PREFIX = "Not reproducible: "
@

A missing link is printed without the [[FOUND-VIA:]] token, under
[[Not reproducible:]], so that nothing in the output can be pasted or
grepped into a provenance block as if it were a verified search.  The
message says why there is no command and, when there are candidate
searches, how to get one: running a search again with [[-n]] records the
link (\cref{sec:review-found-by}).  So we print each candidate as the
exact command to run, with its stored filters and limit and the [[-n]]
of the session.  Exactness matters twice: the search cache is keyed by
query, limit and filters, so only the exact search is answered without a
request to the provider; and a re-run's search record replaces the stored
one, so a different limit would change what later FOUND-VIA lines print.

<<prov command>>=
def _missing_link_lines(record: dict) -> list[str]:
    """Explain why a provenance record has no command."""
    provider = record["provider"]
    candidates = record.get("candidate_commands") or []
    if provider == "snowball":
        return ["added by snowballing, not returned by a search."]
    if len(candidates) < 2:
        return ["the session did not record which search returned it."]
    session_flag = f"-n {_quote_arg(record['session'])}"
    return [
        f"the session did not record which of its {len(candidates)} "
        f"{provider} searches returned it.",
        "Re-run them exactly as below to record it "
        "(a cached search sends no request):",
    ] + [f"  {command} {session_flag}" for command in candidates]
@

Let's verify the heart of the command: that the [[FOUND-VIA]] line carries the
exact stored provider query for the provider that surfaced the paper, as a
[[scholar search]] command and never as a [[scholar rq]] one.

<<test functions>>=
class TestProvCommand:
    """Tests for the prov found-via command."""

    <<prov test methods>>
@

The tests share one fixture builder that saves a session containing a single
paper under a chosen provider.  Its decision status defaults to kept but is
overridable, so a test can check that provenance is reported regardless of the
keep/discard decision; the optional [[filters]]/[[limit]] are applied to each
search record so a test can check that the reconstructed command carries the
flags:

<<prov test methods>>=
def _make_session(
    self, *, name, providers, pairs, paper, provider,
    research_context=None, status="kept", filters=None, limit=None,
):
    """Persist a session containing ``paper`` found via ``provider``."""
    from scholar.review import (
        ReviewDecision, ReviewSession, DecisionStatus, SearchRecord,
        save_session,
    )
    session = ReviewSession(
        query=name,
        providers=providers,
        timestamp=datetime(2026, 3, 25, 14, 49),
        name=name,
        research_context=research_context,
        search_records=[
            SearchRecord(q, p, filters, limit) for q, p in pairs
        ],
    )
    session.decisions.append(
        ReviewDecision(
            paper=paper,
            provider=provider,
            status=DecisionStatus[status.upper()],
            tags=["theme"],
        )
    )
    save_session(session)
@

<<prov test methods>>=
def test_found_via_uses_stored_provider_query(
    self, tmp_path, monkeypatch
):
    """FOUND-VIA reproduces the search, not the LLM-driven rq command."""
    monkeypatch.setenv("SCHOLAR_DATA_DIR", str(tmp_path))
    paper = Paper(
        title="Novice LLMs", authors=["Alice"], year=2024, doi="10.1/llm"
    )
    self._make_session(
        name="llm-novice-programming",
        providers=["openalex", "dblp"],
        pairs=[
            ('"large language models" novice', "openalex"),
            ("llm novice programming", "dblp"),
        ],
        paper=paper,
        provider="openalex",
        research_context="How do LLMs support novice programming?",
    )

    result = runner.invoke(app, ["prov", "found-via", paper.id])

    assert result.exit_code == 0
    assert (
        "FOUND-VIA: scholar search "
        "'\"large language models\" novice' -p openalex"
    ) in result.output
    assert "scholar rq" not in result.output
    assert "-p dblp" not in result.output
