\subsection{Examples left out for their category}

The classifier leaves a confirmed decision out of its examples when
the decision's category does not fit its status, such as a discard
still tagged [[supports-claim]] (\cref{sec:example-category} in the
LLM review module).  [[llm classify]] says so only in its log, which a
user rarely reads, so [[llm status]], the command that counts the
examples, counts these too.  It uses the classifier's own
[[contradictory_examples]], so the two cannot disagree.  The count is
the default one, of confirmed decisions: the unreviewed decisions that
[[--include-llm-examples]] adds are judged the same way, but
[[llm status]] has no such option, and those are counted in the log of
the run that uses them.

The row says what it counts, confirmed decisions that are no
examples, rather than \enquote{examples left out}: the row
\enquote{Training examples} above it counts the decisions marked
[[is_example]] (corrections of the classifier), and one record can be
both, a correction whose category contradicts its status being the
very case of issue~\#255.  Read as examples left out of the training
examples, the two rows would seem to overlap; read as what they are,
they do not.  The row stays within an 80-column terminal.

The row appears only when the count is not zero, like the row for
unknown sources, so the table of a session without such records is
unchanged; the JSON always carries [[examples_left_out]], so a script
need not test for the key.  Nothing names the records here: a human's
\enquote{supports, excluded} is a legitimate record (issue~\#89) and
is not to be nagged about, only kept out of the examples.

