\subsection{The display function}

This function selects the appropriate formatter and invokes it:

<<output formatters>>=
<<formatter classes>>

FORMATTERS: dict[str, type] = {
    "table": TableFormatter,
    "csv": CSVFormatter,
    "json": JSONFormatter,
    "bibtex": BibTeXFormatter,
    "bibtex+prov": BibTeXProvFormatter,
}


def display_results(results: list, output_format: str = "table") -> None:
    """
    Display search results in the specified format.

    Args:
        results: List of SearchResult objects to display.
        output_format: One of 'table', 'csv', 'json', 'bibtex', or
            'bibtex+prov'.
    """
    formatter_class = FORMATTERS.get(output_format, TableFormatter)
    formatter = formatter_class()
    formatter.format(results)
@


\subsection{Table formatter}

The table formatter uses Rich to create visually appealing output.
We show the query and provider as context, then list papers with key metadata.
Below an empty table it used to say \enquote{No results found.} whatever
the reason; a provider that failed (\cref{sec:search-result-error}) now
gets its own line with the reason, worded by [[hits_label]] as in every
other view, and so does one that answered only in part.

<<cli imports>>=
from scholar.scholar import hits_label, is_ranked_provider, result_total
from scholar.scholar import result_dates

<<formatter classes>>=
class TableFormatter:
    """Display results as Rich tables."""

    def __init__(self) -> None:
        self.console = Console()

    def format(self, results: list) -> None:
        """Display results in formatted tables."""
        for result in results:
            table = Table(
                title=Text(f"Results for: {result.query}"),
                caption=Text(
                    f"Provider: {result.provider} | {result.timestamp}"
                ),
                box=box.HORIZONTALS,
            )

            table.add_column("Title", style="cyan", no_wrap=False)
            table.add_column("Authors", style="green")
            table.add_column("Year", justify="right")
            table.add_column("DOI", style="dim")

            for paper in result.papers:
                authors = ", ".join(paper.authors[:3])
                if len(paper.authors) > 3:
                    authors += "..."
                table.add_row(
                    Text(paper.title or ""),
                    Text(authors),
                    Text(str(paper.year) if paper.year else ""),
                    Text(paper.doi or ""),
                    end_section=True,
                )

            self.console.print(table)

            ranked = is_ranked_provider(result.provider)
            label = result_hits_label(result)
            if result.error:
                self.console.print(
                    f"[yellow]Results: {escape(label)}[/yellow]"
                )
            elif not result.papers:
                self.console.print("[dim]No results found.[/dim]")
            elif ranked or label != str(len(result.papers)):
                self.console.print(f"[dim]Results: {escape(label)}[/dim]")
            for line in part_hits_lines(result):
                self.console.print(f"[dim]{escape(line)}[/dim]")
@

A count that stopped at the limit is shown with the total the provider
reported and the cap (\enquote{100 of 2,130 (capped at 100)},
[[count_label]] in the [[scholar]] module), so that the table does not
present the first page of an answer as all of it
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/166}).  A merged result
has no total of its own; below it, each provider's search gets a line
with its own count, total and error
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/164}), since the merged
count cannot say which provider answered what.  Both views and the CSV
share the two helpers.  Whether a search was capped is decided for each
provider's own search, never for the merged count: the union of two
providers' three hits each, one shared, is five, and with a limit of
five it would read as \enquote{capped} although neither provider came
near its limit.

<<formatter classes>>=
def result_hits_label(result) -> str:
    """The hits label of a result, with its total and cap if known.

    A merged result is never called capped: its count is the
    deduplicated union, which can equal the limit by chance; each of
    its parts is judged on its own (`part_hits_lines`).
    """
    total, estimated = result_total(result)
    merged = bool(getattr(result, "parts", None))
    return hits_label(
        len(result.papers), result.error,
        ranked=is_ranked_provider(result.provider),
        total=total, limit=None if merged else getattr(result, "limit", None),
        estimated=estimated,
    )


def part_hits_lines(result) -> list[str]:
    """One line per provider search of a merged result; none otherwise."""
    parts = getattr(result, "parts", None)
    if not isinstance(parts, list) or not parts:
        return []
    return [
        f"{part.provider}: {result_hits_label(part)}" for part in parts
    ]
@

