\subsection{BibTeX formatter}

For \LaTeX{} users, we generate BibTeX entries that can be added to a
[[.bib]] file.

The citation keys follow the scheme the review report and the \LaTeX{}
synthesis use (\cref{sec:cite-keys} in the review module): the first
author's surname reduced to letters and digits, then the year or [[nd]], and a
letter suffix [[a]], [[b]], \dots{} for a second paper with the same key.
This formatter used to build keys its own way, from the last word of the first
author's name with nothing removed, and that went wrong in three ways
(issue~\#122).  \enquote{Smith, John} gave [[john2024]], because the last word
of a surname-first name is the given name.  \enquote{O'Brien} gave
[[o'brien2024]], and biber rejects an apostrophe in a key, which aborts the
whole bibliography.  And two Smiths from 2024 both got [[smith2024]], so the
second entry's key was a duplicate that biber also rejects.  Using the shared
scheme fixes all three and gives a reader one rule for every [[.bib]] Scholar
writes.

The price is that a key in a newly written [[.bib]] can differ from the key
an earlier version of Scholar wrote for the same paper (only for papers that
hit one of the three cases above), so a document citing the old key must be
updated.  The provenance round-trip below is not affected: it matches an entry
to its note by [[Paper.id]], computed from the entry's fields, never by the
key.

A key depends on the papers before it in the output, because only the first
paper with a base key keeps it plain.  The keys are therefore assigned once,
over every paper of every result in output order, by one generator that both
formatters iterate.  The provenance-stub formatter below needs the key for its
comment header \emph{before} the entry itself is rendered, so the generator
hands out each key together with its paper and the entry builder takes the key
as an argument: the header and the entry cannot disagree.  We import the
scheme lazily, as the other commands do with the review module.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _keyed_papers(results: list):
    """Yield `(result, paper, key)` for every paper, keys unique overall."""
    from scholar.review import assign_cite_keys

    pairs = [(result, paper) for result in results for paper in result.papers]
    keys = assign_cite_keys([paper for _, paper in pairs])
    for (result, paper), key in zip(pairs, keys):
        yield result, paper, key
@

The entry builder returns the [[@article]] block as a string instead of
printing it line by line.  A returned string is what lets the provenance-stub
formatter wrap the very same entry in a comment block without re-implementing
any field formatting.

<<formatter classes>>=
class BibTeXFormatter:
    """Output results as BibTeX entries."""

    def format(self, results: list) -> None:
        """Output results as BibTeX."""
        from scholar.review import nonlatin_bib_preamble

        keyed = [
            (result, paper, key, self._format_paper(paper, key))
            for result, paper, key in _keyed_papers(results)
        ]
        preamble = nonlatin_bib_preamble([entry for *_, entry in keyed])
        if preamble:
            print(preamble)
            print()
        for result, paper, key, entry in keyed:
            self._print_entry(result, paper, key, entry)

    def _print_entry(self, result, paper, key: str, entry: str) -> None:
        """Print one rendered `entry` and the blank line after it."""
        print(entry)
        print()

    def _format_paper(self, paper, key: str) -> str:
        """Return `paper` rendered as a BibTeX entry with citation `key`."""
        from scholar.review import escape_bibtex

        authors = " and ".join(paper.authors)
        lines = [
            f"@article{{{key},",
            f"  title = {{{escape_bibtex(paper.title)}}},",
            f"  author = {{{escape_bibtex(authors)}}},",
        ]
        if paper.year:
            lines.append(f"  year = {{{paper.year}}},")
        if paper.doi:
            lines.append(f"  doi = {{{paper.doi}}},")
        if paper.venue:
            lines.append(f"  journal = {{{escape_bibtex(paper.venue)}}},")
        lines.append("}")
        return "\n".join(lines)
@

\label{sec:bibtex-escaping}
The free-text fields go through [[escape_bibtex]], the escaper of the
session export's [[.bib]] (\cref{sec:latex-escaping} in the review
module), so one paper is written one way whichever command wrote it.
The formatter used to copy them as the provider wrote them
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/230}), but a title is
ordinary text, not \LaTeX.  A [[.bib]] of raw titles passed biber, and
then pdfLaTeX stopped on each of these (checked 2026-11-19 with \TeX{}
Live 2025, biblatex, one title per document): [[R&D]]
(\enquote{Misplaced alignment tab character}), [[100% sure]] (the
[[%]] comments out the rest of the entry in the [[.bbl]]: \enquote{File
ended while scanning use of [[\field]]}), [[$5 cost]] and
[[snake_case]] (\enquote{Missing [[$]] inserted}), [[C# code]]
(\enquote{Illegal parameter number}), and a symbol such as U+2606
(white star) or a Greek letter (\enquote{Unicode character \dots{} not
set up for use with LaTeX}).  Curly quotes, dashes and the straight
double quote compiled; the escaper maps them anyway, as it does for
the export.  The DOI and the year are left alone: biblatex's data
model declares [[doi]] a verbatim field ([[blx-dm.def]]), and
escaping its [[_]] would change the link.  The escaped text no longer
hashes to the paper's id when the paper has no DOI;
\cref{sec:prov-paper-id} explains how the provenance commands still
find its note.
@

\paragraph{Text in other scripts.}
The escaper wraps a run of text that pdf\LaTeX{} cannot set in T1, a
Chinese title or a Cyrillic name, in [[\scholarnonlatin{...}]]
(\cref{sec:non-latin-text} in the review module), so that the run is
kept and each engine decides how to show it.  The review's own
documents define the macro; a [[.bib]] that a user cites from a
document of their own does not come with one, and an undefined macro
is an error.  So the output defines it itself, in an [[@preamble]],
which biber copies into the [[.bbl]] that biblatex reads.  The
definition is the review module's ([[nonlatin_definition]]): the text
under Xe\LaTeX{} and Lua\LaTeX{}, the marker under pdf\LaTeX{}, each a
[[\providecommand]], so a definition the user made first wins.  We
checked it by compiling a document that cites such an entry (\TeX{}
Live 2025, biber): pdf\LaTeX{} prints the marker; Xe\LaTeX{} and
Lua\LaTeX{} print the title and the name once the document loads a
font with the scripts (we used Noto Sans CJK SC through
[[fontspec]]), and print nothing for them with the default font, which
has none of the glyphs.

The preamble is written only when an entry uses the macro, once,
before the first entry, so output without such text is byte for byte
what it was.  It is a single line, which keeps it out of the way of
the line scanner of the provenance commands (\cref{sec:prov-bibtex}),
which reads entries, not a preamble.  The review module builds it
([[nonlatin_bib_preamble]], \cref{sec:non-latin-definition}), for this
formatter and for the [[.bib]] files that the session export and the
synthesis write.  We print no hint on standard error: the marker in the
user's document already says that the text is there and not shown, and
a hint on every search would be noise for the scripts that read
[[-f bibtex]].

