\subsection{Provenance-stub BibTeX formatter}
\label{sec:prov-bibtex}

When a written claim is backed by a reference, the citation should travel with
a record of \emph{how it was found}, \emph{why it was picked}, and the
\emph{verbatim quote} that supports the claim.  The most error-prone part of
that record is the [[FOUND-VIA]] line --- a reproducible [[scholar search]]
command --- and that is exactly the part the tool already knows.  This
formatter emits each entry wrapped in a provenance comment block with
[[FOUND-VIA]] pre-filled and the judgement fields left as [[TODO]]
placeholders for the author to complete.

The wrinkle is that by the time results reach a formatter they have passed
through [[SearchResult.merge]], which joins the per-provider queries with
[[" | "]] (every segment is identical, since one query is sent to all
providers) and joins the provider names with [[", "]].  To rebuild a command
the reader can actually run, we undo both joins: collapse the duplicated query
segments back to one query and re-emit a single [[-p]] flag per provider.

Free-text arguments --- the query and the [[--venue]] value --- may contain a
space, a quote, or a shell metacharacter, and the whole point of the line is
that the reader can paste it into a shell and re-run it.  Hand-rolled
double-quoting is not enough: inside double quotes a shell still expands [[$]]
and backticks, so a query like [[term $(rm -rf x)]] would \emph{execute} on
paste rather than be searched for.  We therefore delegate to [[shlex.quote]],
which renders any string as a single shell token (single-quoted when needed,
left bare when already safe) with no metacharacter left live.  The query
goes through the [[_quote_arg]] helper below, the venue through
[[filter_flags]] (\cref{sec:filter-flags} in the [[scholar]] module), and
both are [[shlex.quote]], so the two cannot drift in how they escape.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _quote_arg(value: str) -> str:
    """Return ``value`` quoted as a single safe shell token."""
    return shlex.quote(value)
@

The filters and limit turn back into the flags that produced them; the
filters through [[filter_flags]] of the [[scholar]] module
(\cref{sec:filter-flags}), which the review report shares, and the limit
here.  We take
them as a plain dict and an integer rather than off a result object, because two
callers reconstruct flags from different sources: the [[bibtex+prov]] formatter
reads them off a live [[SearchResult]], while [[prov found-via]] reads them off
a stored [[SearchRecord]].  The [[--limit]] is shown only when it differs from
the default, and the unlimited sentinel maps back to the [[0]] the user actually
typed rather than the large internal cap.  Output and enrichment options such as
[[--enrich]] are deliberately omitted: they change how results are displayed or
augmented, not \emph{which} papers match, so they are irrelevant to reproducing
the found set.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _search_flags(filters: dict | None, limit: int | None) -> list[str]:
    """Reconstruct the filter and limit CLI flags from a filters dict."""
    flags = filter_flags(filters)
    if limit is not None and limit != DEFAULT_SEARCH_LIMIT:
        shown = 0 if limit >= UNLIMITED_PROVIDER_LIMIT else limit
        flags.append(f"--limit {shown}")
    return flags
@

With the helpers in place, the command is just the query, the [[-p]] flags,
and the reconstructed filter flags joined into one line.  The merged joins
reach [[prov found-via]] too: sessions saved before
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/164} stored the merged
result of a multi-provider [[search -n]] as one search record, and their
papers still link to it.  So [[_search_command]] takes the four parts
rather than a result and serves both callers; [[_found_via]] applies it
to a live result.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _search_command(
    query: str, provider: str, filters: dict | None, limit: int | None
) -> str:
    """Reconstruct the `scholar search` command of one search.

    Undoes the `" | "` query join and `", "` provider join introduced by
    `SearchResult.merge`, then re-emits the filter and limit flags, so
    the command reproduces the original search.
    """
    queries = list(dict.fromkeys(query.split(" | ")))
    query = " | ".join(queries)
    providers = [p.strip() for p in provider.split(",") if p.strip()]
    parts = [f"scholar search {_quote_arg(query)}"]
    parts += [f"-p {p}" for p in providers]
    parts += _search_flags(filters, limit)
    return " ".join(parts)


def _found_via(result) -> str:
    """Reconstruct the `scholar search` command behind a result."""
    return _search_command(
        result.query, result.provider, result.filters, result.limit
    )
@

Each paper is preceded by the comment block and then the reused entry.  The
[[DATE]] is the date the database answered the search that surfaced the
paper, not the wall clock.  It used to be the result's [[timestamp]],
the time of the run, so an answer served from the cache was dated
today however old it was
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/167}).  Now it is the
date the answer was fetched (\cref{sec:search-dates} in the [[scholar]]
module), taken from the searches that returned this paper: for a merged
result, the parts in its [[found_in]], so a paper only OpenAlex returned
is dated by OpenAlex's answer.  A cached answer whose fetch date the
cache does not know says so instead of giving a date.  Only a result
that records no dates at all (built by code outside scholar, or by the
tests) falls back to its timestamp's date, as before.  Several dates
are written as a range.

The stub also asks for [[COUNTER]], the search for work that refutes or
qualifies the claim, between [[VERIFIED]] and [[DATE]].  The provenance
checker of the backing-claims skill requires the field, so a stub without
it would fail that check until the line was added by hand
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/176}); and
[[prov import]] must recognise it as a field, or it would end the block and
be kept as personal prose.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _prov_date(result, paper) -> str:
    """The DATE of a provenance block: when the answer was fetched."""
    parts = result.search_parts() if hasattr(result, "search_parts") \
        else [result]
    found_in = getattr(result, "found_in", None) or []
    if len(parts) > 1:
        index = next(
            (i for i, p in enumerate(result.papers) if p is paper), None
        )
        if index is not None and index < len(found_in) and found_in[index]:
            parts = [parts[i] for i in found_in[index]]
    dates, unknown = set(), False
    for part in parts:
        searched, fetched, cached = result_dates(part)
        if fetched:
            dates.add(fetched[:10])
        elif cached:
            unknown = True
        elif searched:
            dates.add(searched[:10])
        elif part.timestamp:
            dates.add(part.timestamp.split("T")[0])
    if not dates:
        return "unknown (cached answer, fetch date not recorded)" \
            if unknown else ""
    date = min(dates) if len(dates) == 1 else \
        f"{min(dates)} to {max(dates)}"
    if unknown:
        date += "; some answers cached, fetch date not recorded"
    return date


class BibTeXProvFormatter(BibTeXFormatter):
    """Output BibTeX entries wrapped in a provenance comment block."""

    def _print_entry(self, result, paper, key: str, entry: str) -> None:
        """Print `entry` below its provenance stub."""
        date = _prov_date(result, paper)
        print(f"% === provenance: {key} ===")
        print(f"% ID: {paper.id}")
        print("% CLAIM: TODO")
        print(f"% FOUND-VIA: {_found_via(result)}")
        print("% PICKED: TODO")
        print("% QUOTE (<loc>): TODO")
        print("% VERIFIED: TODO")
        print("% COUNTER: TODO")
        print(f"% DATE: {date}")
        super()._print_entry(result, paper, key, entry)
@

