\subsection{Parsing a BibTeX file}

We scan the file once, remembering the start of any contiguous run of comment
lines so that when an entry begins we can attach the block(s) directly above it.
A blank or non-comment line resets that memory, so only comments \emph{immediately}
above an entry count as its provenance --- which matches how the formatter emits
them.  The entry's extent is found by brace depth.

<<formatter classes>>=
@dataclass
class ProvEntry:
    """A parsed BibTeX entry with its provenance comment blocks."""

    key: str
    fields: dict
    prov_blocks: list
    comment_start: "int | None"
    entry_start: int
    entry_end: int
@

An entry ends on the line where its brace depth returns to zero.  We only start
counting once a brace has been seen, so a header line such as
[[@article{key,]] is handled the same as a multi-line body.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _entry_end(lines: list, start: int) -> int:
    """Return the index of the line that closes the entry at ``start``."""
    depth = 0
    seen = False
    for index in range(start, len(lines)):
        depth += lines[index].count("{") - lines[index].count("}")
        if "{" in lines[index]:
            seen = True
        if seen and depth <= 0:
            return index
    return len(lines) - 1
@

Field extraction is deliberately narrow: we only need the four fields that
identify a paper, and our own entries write one [[name = {value}]] (or quoted)
field per line.  A line-oriented match avoids mistaking an [[=]] inside a value
for a field assignment.  It assumes each field value is balanced and on one
line --- which the formatter guarantees --- so a value spanning lines or a
literal unbalanced brace is out of scope.

We strip the remaining [[{]] and [[}]] from each value before storing it.  A
[[.bib]] often brace-protects capitalisation, as in [[title = {The {LaTeX}
Companion}]]; left in, those braces would make the title hash to a different
[[Paper.id]] than the brace-free title every provider stores, so an [[import]]
would orphan the paper into a fresh note instead of updating its existing one.
Unwrapping the braces is what lets the no-DOI hash key line up with notes
created elsewhere.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _extract_bib_fields(entry_text: str) -> dict:
    """Pull ``name = {value}`` / ``name = "value"`` fields from an entry."""
    brace = re.compile(r'^\s*(\w+)\s*=\s*\{(.*)\}\s*,?\s*$')
    quote = re.compile(r'^\s*(\w+)\s*=\s*"(.*)"\s*,?\s*$')
    fields = {}
    for line in entry_text.split("\n"):
        match = brace.match(line) or quote.match(line)
        if match:
            fields[match.group(1).lower()] = _bib_field_text(match.group(2))
    return fields
@

BibTeX author fields use [[Last, First]] order joined by [[ and ]].  We
normalise each name to [[First Last]] so the reconstructed [[Paper]] hashes to
the same id the rest of the tool would compute for it.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _split_bib_authors(field: str) -> list:
    """Split a BibTeX ``author`` field into ``First Last`` names."""
    authors = []
    for raw in field.split(" and "):
        raw = raw.strip()
        if not raw:
            continue
        if "," in raw:
            last, first = raw.split(",", 1)
            authors.append(f"{first.strip()} {last.strip()}".strip())
        else:
            authors.append(raw)
    return authors
@

From the fields we build a minimal [[Paper]] --- enough for the notes layer to
derive its id.  We import [[Paper]] lazily so this module keeps its existing
import graph.

A paper with a DOI is identified by it, and the DOI is written verbatim.
A paper without one is identified by a hash of its title and first
author as the provider wrote them, but our own formatters write those
fields escaped (\cref{sec:bibtex-escaping}), as most [[.bib]] files
do: [[R&D]] is [[R\&D]] in the entry, and hashes to another id.  Some
escapes can be undone, but not all: a curly apostrophe and a straight
one are both written [[']], a dash becomes [[--]], and a symbol is
dropped.  So the id cannot be recomputed from the entry in general, and
we stop trying where we can: the provenance block that
[[bibtex+prov]] writes names the paper ([[ID: hash:...]] or
[[ID: doi:...]]), and so does every block that [[prov export]] writes
(\cref{sec:prov-paper-id}).  [[prov import]] reads only entries with a
block, so an entry from either command is keyed by its recorded id,
whatever its title became.

The rest serves entries without the line: [[.bib]] files written before
it, by hand, or by plain [[search -f bibtex]] for [[prov export]].  We
try, in order, the notes that exist, and fall back to the paper the
entry describes:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the id of the fields with the escapes of [[BIBTEX_SPECIALS]]
  undone, which have one meaning each ([[\&]] is [[&]],
  [[\textquotedbl{}]] the double quote; the braces are gone by now),
  and the [[\scholarnonlatin]] wrapper dropped, which keeps the run
  it wraps as it was (\cref{sec:non-latin-text} in the review module).
  Its separator [[\scholarnonlatinsep]], written where a run touches a
  letter, stands for no character of the title and is dropped too,
  first, since the wrapper's name is the beginning of its name;
\item an existing note of a DOI-less paper whose title, escaped as the
  formatter escapes it, is the entry's title ([[_noted_titles]]), and
  whose id that title and a spelling of the entry's first author give.
  The author is escaped too ([[Conan O’Brien]] is written
  [[Conan O'Brien]]), so we try each spelling the escaper writes alike
  ([[_unescaped_spellings]]).  Escaping the note's title rather than
  inverting the entry's works for any escaper, also one that maps more
  characters later;
\item the id of the text as written, braces stripped.  Before the
  escaping, an import from a hand-escaped [[.bib]] keyed its note so
  ([[R\&D in practice]]); taking that note continues it instead of
  starting a second one.
\end{enumerate}
The title index lists every note, so it is built only when an entry
gets that far, once per command.

The notes layer reads only a paper's id and title (and its DOI and
authors when present), so an entry keyed by a recorded hash id needs no
[[Paper]], only a [[_NoteKey]] that carries the two.

<<formatter classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class _NoteKey:
    """The id and title under which the notes layer files a paper."""

    id: str
    title: str


def _bib_field_text(value: str) -> str:
    """Return a field value as the `.bib` parser keeps it: no braces."""
    return value.replace("{", "").replace("}", "")


def _unescape_bib(text: str) -> str:
    """Undo the one-meaning escapes of `escape_bibtex` in a brace-free field.

    Those are the `BIBTEX_SPECIALS`, the `\\scholarnonlatin` wrapper and
    its separator `\\scholarnonlatinsep`.
    """
    from scholar.review import (
        BIBTEX_SPECIALS, NONLATIN_MACRO, NONLATIN_SEP_MACRO,
    )

    text = text.replace(NONLATIN_SEP_MACRO, "").replace(NONLATIN_MACRO, "")
    for char, escaped in BIBTEX_SPECIALS.items():
        text = text.replace(_bib_field_text(escaped), char)
    return text


def _noted_titles() -> dict:
    """Map the escaped title of each DOI-less noted paper to its notes."""
    from scholar.notes import list_papers_with_notes
    from scholar.review import escape_bibtex

    index: dict = {}
    for note in list_papers_with_notes():
        if note.paper_id.startswith("hash:") and note.title:
            key = _bib_field_text(escape_bibtex(note.title))
            index.setdefault(key, []).append(note)
    return index


def _recorded_id(entry) -> "str | None":
    """Return the paper id an entry's provenance block records, if any."""
    for _, fields in entry.prov_blocks:
        if fields.get("ID"):
            return fields["ID"]
    return None


def _paper_from_entry(
    fields: dict, paper_id: "str | None" = None, noted=None
):
    """Return what the notes layer keys an entry's note by.

    `paper_id` is the id the entry's provenance block records; it wins.
    Without it, a DOI-less entry is matched to an existing note (see the
    module documentation); `noted` is a callable returning
    `_noted_titles()`.
    """
    from scholar.notes import get_note
    from scholar.scholar import Paper

    year_raw = fields.get("year", "")
    year = int(year_raw) if year_raw.isdigit() else None
    paper = Paper(
        title=_unescape_bib(fields.get("title", "")),
        authors=_split_bib_authors(_unescape_bib(fields.get("author", ""))),
        year=year,
        doi=fields.get("doi"),
    )
    if paper.doi:
        <<warn when the recorded id is not the entry's DOI>>
        return paper
    recorded = <<the paper the recorded id names>>
    for candidate in (recorded, paper):
        if candidate is not None and get_note(candidate) is not None:
            return candidate
    <<match a noted paper whose title escapes to the entry's>>
    as_written = Paper(
        title=fields.get("title", ""),
        authors=_split_bib_authors(fields.get("author", "")),
        year=year,
    )
    if get_note(as_written) is not None:
        return as_written
    return recorded or paper
@

An entry's own DOI is the stronger identity.  The recorded id was
right when the block was written, but a paper can gain a DOI afterwards
(the TUI then moves its note to the DOI), and a block copied by hand
above another entry names the other paper.  Keying by the recorded id
in either case files the claim under the wrong id: a second note under
the stale hash, or the claim on another paper with this entry's title.
So an entry with a DOI is keyed by it, and a recorded id that differs
is reported on the standard error, naming both, for the user to fix the
block.

<<warn when the recorded id is not the entry's DOI>>=
if paper_id and paper_id != paper.id:
    logger.warning(
        "%s: provenance block records %s, the entry's DOI is %s; "
        "using the DOI", fields.get("title", ""), paper_id, paper.id,
    )
@

Without a DOI the recorded id is the best we know, but it can be stale
too: the title or the first author was corrected after the stub was
written, so the hash changed.  So the recorded id is tried like the
other candidates, by whether a note exists under it, before the
fallbacks below; only when no note exists anywhere does a new note
take it, since it is the id the entry was written from.  A recorded DOI
id on an entry without one becomes a [[Paper]] with that DOI, so that
the notes layer still moves a note kept under the paper's old hash id,
as it does for every paper that gains a DOI.

<<the paper the recorded id names>>=
(
    None if not paper_id
    else Paper(title=paper.title, authors=paper.authors, year=year,
               doi=paper_id[len("doi:"):])
    if paper_id.startswith("doi:")
    else _NoteKey(id=paper_id, title=paper.title)
)
@

<<match a noted paper whose title escapes to the entry's>>=
from scholar.utils import legacy_hash_paper_id

first = paper.authors[0] if paper.authors else ""
surname = first.split()[-1] if first.split() else ""
for note in (noted() if noted else {}).get(fields.get("title", ""), []):
    for spelling in _unescaped_spellings(surname):
        if legacy_hash_paper_id(note.title, [spelling]) == note.paper_id:
            return _NoteKey(id=note.paper_id, title=note.title)
@

The escaper writes several characters alike: [[']] stands for itself or
for a curly apostrophe, a modifier apostrophe or a prime, [[--]] for
itself or an en dash, and so on through [[LATEX_UNICODE_PUNCTUATION]].
A surname is short and holds few of them, so we list every spelling
that escapes to it, longest sequence first ([[---]] before [[--]]),
and stop at [[MAX_SPELLINGS]] in case a name is all punctuation.  A
dropped symbol cannot be listed.

<<constants>>=
MAX_SPELLINGS = 64
<<formatter classes>>=
def _unescaped_spellings(text: str) -> list:
    """Return the spellings `escape_bibtex` writes as `text`, text first."""
    from scholar.review import LATEX_UNICODE_PUNCTUATION

    sources: dict = {}
    for char, escaped in LATEX_UNICODE_PUNCTUATION.items():
        written = _bib_field_text(escaped)
        if written:
            sources.setdefault(written, []).append(char)
    pattern = "|".join(
        re.escape(seq) for seq in sorted(sources, key=len, reverse=True)
    )
    spellings = [""]
    for part in re.split(f"({pattern})", text) if pattern else [text]:
        choices = [part] + sources.get(part, [])
        spellings = [s + c for s in spellings for c in choices]
        spellings = spellings[:MAX_SPELLINGS]
    return spellings
@

A comment region may stack several blocks.  We strip the comment markers and
reuse the note splitter, keeping only its provenance segments --- so any stray
non-provenance comment is simply ignored.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _comment_prov_blocks(comment_lines: list) -> list:
    """Extract provenance ``(key, fields)`` blocks from comment lines."""
    stripped = "\n".join(
        _strip_comment_prefix(line) for line in comment_lines
    )
    return [
        payload
        for kind, payload in _split_note_segments(stripped)
        if kind == "prov"
    ]
@

The export rewriter needs the complement: the comment lines that are \emph{not}
part of a provenance block.  A user may keep an ordinary [[%]] note above an
entry, and replacing the provenance region must not take that with it.  We
re-walk the run at the raw-line level --- dropping a header and its field lines,
keeping everything else --- so a personal comment survives an export.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _kept_comment_lines(comment_lines: list) -> list:
    """Return the comment lines that are not part of a provenance block."""
    kept = []
    in_block = False
    for line in comment_lines:
        stripped = _strip_comment_prefix(line)
        if PROV_HEADER_RE.match(stripped):
            in_block = True
            continue
        if in_block and (
            _is_prov_field_line(stripped) or _is_continuation_line(stripped)
        ):
            continue
        in_block = False
        kept.append(line)
    return kept
@

The scanner ties these together: it tracks the pending comment run, and on each
entry records the bibkey, the identifying fields, the attached blocks, and the
line spans the rewriter will need.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _parse_bib_entries(text: str) -> list:
    """Parse a ``.bib`` into entries with their provenance comment blocks."""
    lines = text.split("\n")
    entries = []
    comment_start = None
    index = 0
    while index < len(lines):
        line = lines[index]
        if line.strip().startswith("%"):
            if comment_start is None:
                comment_start = index
            index += 1
            continue
        match = BIB_ENTRY_RE.match(line)
        if match:
            entry_end = _entry_end(lines, index)
            entry_text = "\n".join(lines[index:entry_end + 1])
            blocks = (
                _comment_prov_blocks(lines[comment_start:index])
                if comment_start is not None else []
            )
            entries.append(ProvEntry(
                key=match.group(1),
                fields=_extract_bib_fields(entry_text),
                prov_blocks=blocks,
                comment_start=comment_start,
                entry_start=index,
                entry_end=entry_end,
            ))
            comment_start = None
            index = entry_end + 1
            continue
        comment_start = None
        index += 1
    return entries
@

Let's verify the scanner attaches a stacked pair of blocks to the entry below
them and recovers the entry's fields:

