\section{The Provenance Round-Trip Commands}

With the layers in place, the commands are thin.  They attach to the [[prov]]
group introduced in \cref{sec:prov-commands} rather than defining a second
one---a single [[prov_app]] keeps [[found-via]], [[import]], and [[export]]
under one application.

\subsection{Importing blocks into notes}

[[import]] reads each entry's blocks, merges them into the paper's note by
claim, and saves the result.  Reading the existing note first --- and merging
rather than overwriting --- is what preserves both prior claims and the user's
own prose.  Entries without a block are skipped so we never create an empty
note.

\label{sec:prov-paper-id}
A block records the id of its paper on an [[ID]] line, which
[[bibtex+prov]] writes from the paper and [[prov export]] from the note
it found, so the entry and its note meet whatever the escaping did to
the entry's title (\cref{sec:bib-escape-round-trip}).  The line belongs
to the [[.bib]], not to the note: the note is filed under that id
already, and a stored copy could only disagree with it after the paper
gains a DOI.  So an import drops it from the blocks it stores, and an
export writes the note's current id into every block it emits.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _without_ids(blocks) -> list:
    """Return provenance `(key, fields)` blocks without their ID field."""
    return [
        (key, {name: value for name, value in fields.items()
               if name != "ID"})
        for key, fields in blocks
    ]
@

The note's title is the provider's, which the entry holds only escaped
(\enquote{Developers' ``view''} for \enquote{Developers’ “view”}).
Saving the note under the entry's text would replace the better title,
so an existing note keeps its own; only a new note takes the entry's.

