\section{Interactive Tutorials}
\label{sec:tutorial-command}

The README and this document tell a newcomer what the commands do, but
neither makes them \emph{do} anything.
The [[tutorial]] subcommand embeds pytorial, an interactive tutorial
runner: each step opens a shell in a per-run workspace, the learner types
the command the step asks for, and the transcript is checked against the
step's required patterns before the lesson moves on.
That is the try-first-tell-later arc the rest of this project uses, applied
to the tool itself.
The lessons are literate sources under [[src/scholar/tutorials/]]; each
tangles to the Markdown pytorial reads and weaves to a chapter of this
document that explains the lesson's design (the Tutorials part).

pytorial's embedding entry point mounts only what the host hands it,
prepends its own [[using-tutorials]] lesson (how the runner itself works),
hides the standalone-only [[install]] command and ignores the user's
installed-tutorial directory, so [[scholar tutorial]] serves exactly what
Scholar ships.
We hand it the tangled files by name, in lesson order: a directory would
be listed alphabetically, and the order matters because the later lessons
assume the earlier ones (the LLM lesson assumes the learner has seen a
session; the agent lesson assumes decisions and exports).
The existence filter is not defensive decoration: pytorial raises when a
configured path is missing, so a source checkout that has not been tangled
would otherwise break every [[scholar tutorial]] call instead of merely
listing fewer lessons.

<<tutorial command>>=
TUTORIAL_DIR = Path(__file__).with_name("tutorials")
TUTORIAL_FILES = (
    "first-search.md",
    "reviewing-in-the-tui.md",
    "enrich-verify-export.md",
    "llm-synthesis.md",
    "scholar-with-an-agent.md",
)


def bundled_tutorial_paths() -> tuple[Path, ...]:
    """Return the tangled tutorial files that exist, in lesson order.

    An untangled source checkout mounts nothing rather than breaking
    every `scholar tutorial` call.
    """
    candidates = (TUTORIAL_DIR / name for name in TUTORIAL_FILES)
    return tuple(path for path in candidates if path.exists())
@

\subsection{Sandboxing the tutorial shell}

A lesson that creates review sessions must not leave them in the learner's
real session list, and running a lesson twice must start from the same
state both times.
Both follow from giving the tutorial shell its own [[SCHOLAR_DATA_DIR]]
inside pytorial's workspace: workspaces are created per run, so
[[tutorial run --restart]] is a clean slate, and a step that asks the
learner to run [[scholar sessions list]] can promise exactly which sessions
appear.
The search cache is deliberately \emph{not} sandboxed---it holds provider
responses, not user state, and sharing it makes a repeated run fast and
mostly offline.
The price is that the rate-limit bookkeeping of \cref{ch:ratelimit}, which
also lives in the data directory, starts empty in every run; the lessons
therefore only drive keyless providers, where that memory does not matter.

The shell must also find the same [[scholar]] that started the tutorial.
Under pipx it is on the user's [[PATH]] already, but a developer running
[[uv run scholar tutorial run ...]] gets a nested bash whose [[PATH]] does
not include the project's virtual environment.
Prepending the running interpreter's directory covers both cases (and
brings the [[llm]] command along, which the LLM lesson needs).

<<tutorial command>>=
def tutorial_shell_environment(workspace: str | Path) -> dict[str, str]:
    """Return the environment overrides for a tutorial shell in `workspace`.

    Scholar's data directory is kept inside the workspace, so tutorial
    sessions never reach the user's real session list, and the running
    interpreter's directory is put first on `PATH` so the nested shell
    finds the same `scholar` (and `llm`) that started the tutorial.
    """
    data_dir = Path(workspace) / "scholar-data"
    data_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    bin_dir = str(Path(sys.executable).parent)
    path = os.environ.get("PATH", "")
    return {
        "SCHOLAR_DATA_DIR": str(data_dir),
        "PATH": bin_dir if not path else f"{bin_dir}{os.pathsep}{path}",
    }
@

pytorial builds the shell's environment by copying the process environment
and updating it with whatever [[env]] the runner passes, so the overrides
above are an overlay, not a replacement.
We also apply them to the process environment itself.
A step's [[check_command]] and [[post_command]] hooks run as plain
subprocesses of the [[scholar]] process, not inside the tutorial shell, and
would otherwise look at the learner's real session store.
The [[**kwargs]] is load-bearing: pytorial inspects the runner's signature
and forwards the per-tutorial prompt name and any [[pre_command]] only to a
runner that accepts them, so a wrapper with a fixed signature would silently
lose both.

<<tutorial command>>=
def tutorial_shell_runner(*, workspace: str | Path, **kwargs):
    """Open pytorial's interactive shell with Scholar sandboxed in `workspace`."""
    overrides = tutorial_shell_environment(workspace)
    os.environ.update(overrides)
    kwargs.pop("env", None)
    return pytorial.shell.run_interactive_shell(
        workspace=workspace, env=overrides, **kwargs
    )
@

The subcommand is mounted at import time, like the other command groups.
The [[scholar]] namespace keeps pytorial's progress records apart from
those of a standalone pytorial installation, and [[allow_shell]] is on
because the only tutorials ever mounted are the ones shipped here.

<<tutorial command>>=
pytorial.cli.add_typer_subcommand(
    app,
    name="tutorial",
    namespace="scholar",
    tutorial_paths=bundled_tutorial_paths(),
    shell_runner=tutorial_shell_runner,
    allow_shell=True,
)
<<cli imports>>=
import os
import sys

import pytorial.cli
import pytorial.shell
@

pytorial defers its heavy imports to the point of use precisely so that a
host can import it on every invocation, so a plain import costs little;
a guarded import would only turn a broken installation into a silently
missing subcommand.

Let's verify the wiring.
The first test is the guard for the others: with an untangled checkout the
parametrised test below would collect nothing and pass.

