\section{Lazily loaded subcommands}
\label{sec:lazy-subcommands}

Two command groups come from separate projects, included as git
submodules: [[scholar snowball]] (snowballing reviews) and
[[scholar tuxedo]] (organising papers with LLMs).
Each package offers one hook, [[register_commands(app)]], which adds its
commands to a Typer app.
That hook is all Scholar relies on: nothing here knows how either package
builds its commands.

Both packages are expensive to import.
Importing [[tuxedo]] imports the [[openai]] client, and importing
[[snowball]] imports [[pandas]] and its Textual TUI;
[[python -X importtime]] put them at about 0.7~s and 0.9~s (November 2026).
Scholar used to import both and call their hooks in [[main]], before the
command line was parsed, so every command paid for them:
[[scholar cache path]] took about 2.8~s and [[scholar --help]] about 3.4~s,
although neither uses snowball or tuxedo
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/130}).
Agents and scripts call [[scholar]] many times in a row, so the fixed cost
adds up to minutes.

We therefore import a submodule only when its subcommand runs.
To do that we need to know when Click needs which part of a command.
A Click group is asked about its subcommands in three situations:
\begin{description}
\item[Help] [[scholar --help]] lists every subcommand with its short help.
  The help formatter calls the group's [[list_commands]] and then
  [[get_command]] for each name.
\item[Dispatch] [[scholar snowball init]] needs the real command, with its
  callback, options and subcommands.
  The group's [[invoke]] gets it from [[resolve_command]].
\item[Completion] Completing [[scholar sn]] lists names and short help, as
  help does, through [[list_commands]] and [[get_command]].
  Completing [[scholar snowball ]] must descend into the real command, and
  Click's completion resolver descends with [[resolve_command]].
\end{description}
Help and name completion need only a name and a line of text, while
dispatch and descent both pass through [[resolve_command]].
So the group lists a placeholder for each submodule, a command that has the
help text but no code, and its [[resolve_command]] replaces the placeholder
with the real command, importing the submodule at that moment.
The rest of this section builds the pieces: the help texts, how we find a
submodule without importing it, how we load the real command, and the group
that ties them together.

\subsection{The listed help texts}

The placeholders need the help line that [[scholar --help]] shows, and the
only way to get it from a package is to import it.
So we keep a copy here, keyed by subcommand name; the key order is the
order in which the subcommands are listed, after Scholar's own commands.
A copy can drift from the original, so a test below compares each entry
with the help of the real command.

<<lazy subcommand group>>=
LAZY_SUBCOMMANDS: dict[str, str] = {
    "snowball": "Systematic literature review using snowballing.",
    "tuxedo": "Organize literature review papers with LLMs.",
}
@

\subsection{Finding a submodule without importing it}

A submodule can be installed in two ways: nested in Scholar as
[[scholar.<name>]] or as a sibling package [[<name>]].
We look for both, nested first.
[[importlib.util.find_spec]] asks the import system's finders where a
module is, without running it.
For [[scholar.<name>]] it imports the parent package [[scholar]], which is
already loaded; the help test below checks that neither submodule is
imported.
Its docstring says it raises [[ValueError]] for a module in
[[sys.modules]] without a spec, and a missing parent raises
[[ModuleNotFoundError]]; both mean that this candidate is not the one we
want.

The old registration tried [[import scholar.<name>]] and then
[[import <name>]] and ignored every [[ImportError]].
That made a package that is installed but fails to import (a missing
dependency, say) look exactly like an absent one: its command silently
disappeared from [[scholar --help]].
Deciding by the spec separates the two cases.
A submodule without a spec is not listed at all; one with a spec is listed,
and if its import fails, running it says why (see below).

<<lazy subcommand group>>=
def find_submodule(name: str) -> Optional[str]:
    """Return the module that provides subcommand `name`, or None.

    Tries `scholar.<name>` (nested package) before `<name>` (sibling
    package). Nothing is imported apart from the parent package.
    """
    for module_name in (f"scholar.{name}", name):
        try:
            if importlib.util.find_spec(module_name) is not None:
                return module_name
        except (ImportError, ValueError):
            continue
    return None
@

<<cli imports>>=
import importlib
import importlib.util
@

\subsection{Loading the real command}

To get the real command we import the module and let its own
[[register_commands]] add the command to a scratch Typer app.
Typer turns that app into a Click group, and the command we want is the
group's entry under [[name]].
Going through the hook rather than importing, say,
[[snowball.cli.app]] directly keeps us on the packages' public contract:
snowball registers a Typer sub-app, tuxedo a single command that hands its
arguments to its own Click group, and we need not know which.

A module that imports but has no [[register_commands]] hook, or whose
hook does not register [[name]], is as unusable as one that does not
import, so we report it with the same exception type and let the caller
handle all three alike.
The missing hook is not hypothetical: [[find_spec]] also finds a plain
directory of that name (a namespace package), such as an uninitialised
submodule directory in the current directory.

<<lazy subcommand group>>=
def load_lazy_subcommand(name: str) -> TyperCommand | TyperGroup:
    """Import the submodule behind subcommand `name` and return its command.

    Raises ImportError when the submodule cannot be found or imported, or
    when its `register_commands` does not register a command `name`.
    """
    module_name = find_submodule(name)
    if module_name is None:
        raise ImportError(f"no module scholar.{name} or {name}")
    module = importlib.import_module(module_name)
    register = getattr(module, "register_commands", None)
    if register is None:
        raise ImportError(f"{module_name} has no register_commands")
    scratch = typer.Typer()
    register(scratch)
    command = typer.main.get_group(scratch).commands.get(name)
    if command is None:
        raise ImportError(f"{module_name} registers no command {name!r}")
    return command
@

<<cli imports>>=
from typer.core import TyperCommand, TyperGroup
@

\subsection{The group}

The placeholder is its own class, so that [[resolve_command]] can tell it
from Scholar's own commands by type.
It has no callback: it is never run, because every path that would run it
goes through [[resolve_command]] first.

<<lazy subcommand group>>=
class LazySubcommandStub(TyperCommand):
    """Placeholder for a submodule subcommand that is not imported yet."""
@

The group adds a placeholder for each submodule that can be found, after
Typer has added Scholar's own commands, so the submodules are listed last.
This runs on every invocation, which is fine because [[find_submodule]]
imports nothing; the help test below checks exactly that.

In [[resolve_command]] we let Typer resolve the name first, which keeps its
\enquote{No such command} error and its spelling suggestions.
Only when the result is a placeholder do we load the real command and return
it in the placeholder's stead.
Both callers use the command they get back, Typer's [[invoke]] to run it and
the completion resolver to descend into it, so the group need not remember
it.
If the import fails, [[ctx.fail]] ends the run with a usage error (exit
code~2) whose one-line message names the subcommand and the reason, instead
of a traceback.
During completion, [[ctx.resilient_parsing]] is set, and a completion must
never print errors into the user's shell.
There we keep the placeholder: it has no options or subcommands, so
completion offers nothing after the name.
Resolving to no command instead would make Click complete from the parent
group and offer Scholar's own command names in that position.

<<lazy subcommand group>>=
class LazySubcommandGroup(TyperGroup):
    """A Typer group that imports submodule subcommands only when used."""

    def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs) -> None:
        super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)
        for name, help_text in LAZY_SUBCOMMANDS.items():
            if name not in self.commands and find_submodule(name):
                self.commands[name] = LazySubcommandStub(name, help=help_text)

    def resolve_command(self, ctx, args):
        """Resolve as Typer does, replacing a placeholder by its command."""
        cmd_name, cmd, rest = super().resolve_command(ctx, args)
        if isinstance(cmd, LazySubcommandStub):
            try:
                cmd = load_lazy_subcommand(cmd_name)
            except ImportError as error:
                if ctx.resilient_parsing:
                    return cmd_name, cmd, rest
                ctx.fail(f"{cmd_name} is unavailable: {error}")
        return cmd_name, cmd, rest
@

The application is created with this group class ([[cls=]] in
\cref{sec:typer-application}), which is why [[<<lazy subcommand group>>]]
is tangled before [[<<typer application>>]].

\subsection{Testing the lazy subcommands}

The property we want, \enquote{[[scholar --help]] imports neither
submodule}, cannot be checked inside the test process: the other tests
import both packages, and once a module is in [[sys.modules]] it stays
there.
So we run the CLI in a fresh interpreter, through [[main]] rather than
[[app]], so that an eager import added back to [[main]] (as
[[register_submodules]] used to be) also fails the test.
The child prints to stderr which heavy modules it ended up importing.
We list [[openai]] besides [[tuxedo]] because it is what made tuxedo slow,
and [[scholar.crossref]] because [[<<currency reporting helpers>>]] used to
import it at module level (\cref{sec:currency-reporting}).
The child inherits the environment, so it uses the test's temporary cache
and data directories rather than the developer's; it prints the cache
directory it saw, and the tests check it.
We assert on which modules were imported, not on time, so the test does
not depend on the machine's speed.

<<test functions>>=
import os
import subprocess
import sys

HEAVY_MODULES = ("tuxedo", "snowball", "openai", "scholar.crossref")
CLI_CHILD_CODE = f"""
import os, sys
sys.argv = ["scholar", *sys.argv[1:]]
import scholar.cli
try:
    scholar.cli.main()
except SystemExit:
    pass
loaded = [name for name in {HEAVY_MODULES!r} if name in sys.modules]
print("LOADED:" + ",".join(loaded), file=sys.stderr)
print("CACHE:" + os.environ["SCHOLAR_CACHE_DIR"], file=sys.stderr)
"""


def run_cli_in_child(*args, env=None):
    """Run `scholar <args>` through `main()` in a fresh interpreter.

    Returns the completed process, the stdout of the CLI and the list of
    heavy modules the run imported.
    """
    process = subprocess.run(
        [sys.executable, "-c", CLI_CHILD_CODE, *args],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        env=env,
        timeout=120,
    )
    lines = process.stderr.splitlines()
    loaded = next(line for line in lines if line.startswith("LOADED:"))
    cache = next(line for line in lines if line.startswith("CACHE:"))
    assert cache.removeprefix("CACHE:") == os.environ["SCHOLAR_CACHE_DIR"]
    modules = [name for name in loaded.removeprefix("LOADED:").split(",")
               if name]
    return process, process.stdout, modules


class TestLazySubcommands:
    """Snowball and tuxedo are listed without being imported."""

    <<lazy subcommand test methods>>
